import Foundation

enum AppBuilderQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A company needs to generate a formatted PDF quote from Opportunity data whenever a rep clicks a button, including a logo and multi-page tables. No declarative tool natively renders PDFs. What is the best recommendation?", options: [("A", "Install an AppExchange document generation package (e.g., Conga Composer or similar) that integrates with Salesforce data to produce the PDF, or build a custom Visualforce page with renderAs=\"pdf\"."), ("B", "Use a Roll-Up Summary field to concatenate the quote text into a single field for printing."), ("C", "Build a record-triggered Flow with a Create Records element that outputs a PDF file."), ("D", "Use a Screen Flow with a Display Text component to show the quote, then ask users to print their screen.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce has no native declarative PDF generation; the standard paths are an AppExchange document generation package or a Visualforce page rendered as PDF, both of which are explicitly designed for this exact requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Flow's Create Records element creates Salesforce records, not binary PDF files; there is no native Flow action for PDF rendering.\n* B: Roll-up summary fields only aggregate numeric values (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) from child records on a master-detail relationship; they cannot generate formatted documents.\n* D: Asking users to manually print their screen is not a scalable, reliable business solution and produces no consistent branded output."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A business process must iterate over hundreds of child Order Line Items on save, apply complex conditional discount logic across several fields, and update a summary field, all within a single transaction with governor-limit safety. Which tool is appropriate, and why?", options: [("A", "A validation rule that blocks saving until the discount logic is manually verified."), ("B", "An Apex trigger (or Apex class invoked by a trigger), since it natively bulkifies operations, offers fine-grained governor limit control, and can implement complex conditional logic across many records reliably."), ("C", "A formula field on the Order object referencing each line item individually."), ("D", "A Roll-Up Summary field configured with a MAX aggregate on the discount field."), ("E", "A Screen Flow, since screens allow the user to manually review each line item before saving.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Apex natively bulkifies operations and gives fine control over governor limits, making it the right tool for complex, high-volume, conditional processing across many child records in a single transaction — this exceeds Flow Builder's practical boundaries. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* E: A Screen Flow requires manual user interaction per screen, which doesn't scale to hundreds of records processed automatically on save.\n* C: A formula field cannot iterate over multiple child records with complex conditional business logic; it only calculates from directly related fields.\n* D: A Roll-Up Summary field only performs simple aggregation (COUNT/SUM/MIN/MAX) on a single field; it cannot apply complex conditional discount logic."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "A mid-size company wants to add e-signature capability to its quote-to-close process without custom development. What is the most appropriate recommendation?", options: [("A", "Use a Rich Text Area field where users type their name as a substitute for a signature."), ("B", "Use a Roll-Up Summary field to track whether a document was signed."), ("C", "Install a well-reviewed AppExchange e-signature package (e.g., DocuSign) that integrates natively with Salesforce records and has passed Salesforce Security Review."), ("D", "Write a custom Lightning Web Component that captures a mouse-drawn signature and stores it as a Base64 text field.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: E-signature is a well-established AppExchange category with security-reviewed, natively-integrated packages; using one avoids custom development risk and delivers a legally recognized signature capture experience out of the box. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Building a custom signature-capture LWC from scratch duplicates a mature capability already available and security-reviewed on AppExchange, at higher cost and risk.\n* A: Typing a name in a text field provides no actual signature capture or legal signature validity, failing the core business requirement.\n* B: A Roll-Up Summary field only aggregates numeric values from child records; it cannot track a boolean signing status meaningfully or capture a signature."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "Choose 2. A company is evaluating whether specific requirements can be met declaratively or require an AppExchange package or code. Which two requirements are examples of scenarios best solved by installing an AppExchange package rather than building custom Apex? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Generating professional branded quote PDFs with complex multi-page tables and dynamic pricing tiers."), ("B", "Adding a validation rule that requires Close Date to be in the future for new Opportunities."), ("C", "Integrating with a legally compliant, widely adopted e-signature workflow for contracts."), ("D", "Displaying the Stage field value in the record highlights panel."), ("E", "Creating a required picklist field for Opportunity Type.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: Both branded PDF generation with dynamic pricing and legally compliant e-signature workflows are mature, well-established AppExchange categories that are impractical to build from scratch and are best solved by installing a security-reviewed package. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: A future-dated Close Date requirement is a simple declarative validation rule, not a scenario requiring AppExchange.\n* D: Displaying a field in the highlights panel is a basic compact layout configuration, fully declarative with no package needed.\n* E: Creating a required picklist field is a standard declarative field configuration task, not an AppExchange use case."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "An Opportunity object has OWD set to Private. A regional sales director needs visibility into all Opportunities owned by every rep in their region, but not opportunities from other regions, without writing code. What is the best solution?", options: [("A", "Set OWD to Public Read/Write for the entire org so everyone can see every opportunity."), ("B", "Ask each rep to manually share every Opportunity record with the director one at a time."), ("C", "Grant the sales director the \"Modify All Data\" system permission via their profile."), ("D", "Enable \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" (already the platform default for Opportunity) and position the director's role above their region's reps in the role hierarchy, so access flows upward automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: With Private OWD and hierarchy-based access enabled, positioning the director above their region's reps in the role hierarchy automatically grants visibility to all opportunities owned by those reps, without exposing records from unrelated regions. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: \"Modify All Data\" grants access to literally every record in the org, including other regions' opportunities, violating the requirement to restrict visibility to just their region.\n* A: Setting OWD to Public Read/Write exposes every opportunity org-wide to every user, which is far broader than the stated requirement.\n* B: Manual sharing record-by-record does not scale, is not automated for new records, and is an inappropriate solution for an ongoing organizational reporting need."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "A user has \"Read\" object permission on Accounts via their profile, but a permission set assigned to them explicitly grants \"Edit\" on Accounts. What is the user's effective object-level access to Accounts?", options: [("A", "Edit access (which includes Read), because permission sets are additive and the most permissive combination of profile and all assigned permission sets always wins."), ("B", "No access at all, because conflicting permissions between a profile and permission set cancel each other out."), ("C", "Read only, because the profile is always the final authority on object permissions."), ("D", "The system prompts the admin to manually choose which permission takes precedence.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce combines profile and permission set permissions using a most-permissive-wins model; since permission sets can only add access, not remove it, the Edit grant from the permission set applies and includes Read. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Profiles are not the final unconditional authority — permission sets are specifically designed to layer additional access on top of the profile.\n* B: Conflicting permissions do not cancel out; Salesforce's access model has no \"cancellation\" mechanism for object permissions.\n* D: There is no manual prompt or admin decision point at runtime; the platform resolves this deterministically and automatically."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "A user with the Sales Rep profile needs the ability to delete Opportunities for exactly one quarter while covering for a colleague on leave, after which the ability should be revoked cleanly. What is the recommended approach?", options: [("A", "Add the \"Delete\" permission on Opportunity directly to the Sales Rep profile for the duration."), ("B", "Create and assign a permission set granting \"Delete\" on Opportunity to that specific user, then remove the permission set assignment when the coverage period ends."), ("C", "Change the user's profile entirely to System Administrator for the quarter."), ("D", "Grant \"Modify All Data\" to the user for the quarter to cover all possible needs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A permission set assigned to just the one user grants the needed temporary access without affecting every other Sales Rep on the shared profile, and it can be cleanly removed afterward — exactly matching the least-privilege, temporary nature of the request. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Modifying the shared Sales Rep profile grants delete access to every user on that profile, not just the one covering for a colleague, an unnecessarily broad change.\n* C: Switching to System Administrator grants vastly more access than needed and changes numerous other settings (login hours, IP ranges, app visibility) beyond just delete rights.\n* D: \"Modify All Data\" is an extremely broad, org-wide permission far exceeding the narrow, temporary need described."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "A dashboard needs to show each of 50 sales reps their own personal pipeline data when they view it, without the admin creating 50 separate dashboards. What feature satisfies this?", options: [("A", "Fifty individual dashboards, one cloned and filtered per rep."), ("B", "A single report emailed manually to each rep every morning."), ("C", "A dynamic dashboard configured to \"Run as logged-in user,\" so each viewer automatically sees data scoped to their own record access."), ("D", "A static dashboard with \"Run as specified user\" set to the sales manager.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A dynamic dashboard set to run as the logged-in user shows each viewer their own data based on their own record access, letting one dashboard serve all 50 reps individually without the admin building duplicate dashboards. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: \"Run as specified user\" always shows that one specified user's data to everyone, the opposite of a personalized per-viewer experience.\n* A: Cloning 50 separate dashboards is exactly the manual, unscalable duplication the requirement is trying to avoid.\n* B: Manually emailing reports every morning is not an automated, real-time interactive dashboard solution."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "A report needs to show all Accounts that have zero related Opportunities, to identify prospecting gaps. Which reporting feature answers this \"without\" question?", options: [("A", "A joined report combining an Account block and an Opportunity block."), ("B", "A summary formula column calculating opportunity count."), ("C", "A bucket field grouping Accounts by revenue size."), ("D", "A cross filter on the Account report: \"Accounts WITHOUT Opportunities.\"")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A cross filter is specifically designed to answer \"with/without related records\" questions — filtering the primary object based on the presence or absence of a related object, exactly matching the \"Accounts without Opportunities\" requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: A bucket field categorizes values into custom groups (like revenue tiers); it has no mechanism for filtering based on the existence of related child records.\n* A: A joined report displays multiple report blocks side-by-side for comparison, but does not filter one object based on the absence of related records in another.\n* B: A summary formula column calculates a value from other report columns; it doesn't filter which Accounts appear based on missing related Opportunities."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "A VP wants a single dashboard showing 25 different KPIs across Sales, Service, and Marketing. What key platform constraint must the app builder account for when designing this?", options: [("A", "A single dashboard supports a maximum of 20 components, so the design must consolidate KPIs into fewer, denser components or split across multiple dashboards."), ("B", "Dashboards can only display data from a single report type, so all 25 KPIs must come from the same report."), ("C", "Dashboards cannot display Service or Marketing data, only Sales data."), ("D", "Dashboards support unlimited components, so all 25 KPIs can go on one dashboard freely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce dashboards have a hard limit of 20 components; a 25-KPI requirement must be addressed by consolidating some metrics into denser visualizations (e.g., a single matrix with multiple columns) or splitting the KPIs across more than one dashboard. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Dashboards are explicitly capped at 20 components; there is no unlimited component capability.\n* B: Each dashboard component is powered by its own independent source report, so a dashboard can combine many different report types across many components.\n* C: Dashboards can display data from Service, Marketing, or any object with underlying reports; there's no platform restriction limiting a dashboard to Sales data only."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "Choose 2. An architect needs to decide between a Summary report and a Matrix report for a specific requirement: showing Opportunity Amount totals broken down by both Sales Rep (rows) and Fiscal Quarter (columns) in a pivot-style grid. Which two statements are accurate? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A Matrix report is the correct format since it supports grouping by both rows and columns simultaneously, matching the pivot-style requirement."), ("B", "A Summary report only supports grouping in one dimension (typically rows), making it insufficient for this specific two-dimensional pivot requirement."), ("C", "A Tabular report is the best choice since it natively supports subtotals by two dimensions."), ("D", "Joined reports are required whenever more than one grouping dimension is needed."), ("E", "Matrix reports cannot include summary formula columns.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Matrix reports are specifically designed for two-dimensional grouping (rows and columns), which is exactly what's needed for a Sales Rep by Fiscal Quarter pivot; Summary reports only group in one dimension, which is insufficient here. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Tabular reports have no grouping at all — they are simple row lists and cannot produce subtotals by any dimension, let alone two.\n* D: Joined reports combine different report types side-by-side for comparison; they are not required simply because two grouping dimensions are needed — Matrix already handles that natively.\n* E: Matrix reports do support summary formula columns, so this statement about a limitation is factually incorrect."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A company wants field sales reps to log a client visit note in three taps from anywhere in the mobile app, without navigating to a specific object first. What is the best solution?", options: [("A", "A custom report scheduled to run daily."), ("B", "A global \"Log a Call\" quick action added to the global publisher layout, making it accessible from the mobile navigation and global action bar regardless of context."), ("C", "An object-specific quick action on the Contact object only."), ("D", "A Chatter group where reps are asked to post their notes as text.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A global action is available anywhere in the app, including the mobile action bar, without requiring the user to first navigate to a specific record or object — exactly matching the \"from anywhere, minimal taps\" requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: An object-specific action on Contact only appears when viewing a Contact record, requiring navigation first, which contradicts the \"from anywhere\" requirement.\n* A: A scheduled report has nothing to do with capturing a quick, in-the-moment note from a mobile action.\n* D: Asking reps to manually post in a Chatter group isn't a structured quick-action workflow and doesn't create a trackable Task record."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "Field sales reps using the mobile app report that action buttons for creating follow-up tasks are buried in an overflow menu, forcing extra taps after every client visit. What page layout configuration should the app builder review and adjust?", options: [("A", "The Compact Layout field order, since compact layouts control which actions appear first."), ("B", "The Field-Level Security settings on the Task object."), ("C", "The order of quick actions in the \"Salesforce Mobile and Lightning Experience Actions\" section of the page layout, since the first actions listed become the visible action bar icons, with the rest going into overflow."), ("D", "The org's default time zone setting.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The order of actions in the page layout's mobile/Lightning actions section directly determines which actions appear as immediately visible icons in the mobile action bar versus which get pushed into the overflow menu — reordering this section surfaces the most-used action. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Compact layouts control which fields display in the highlights panel and record cards, not which quick actions appear in the mobile action bar.\n* B: Field-Level Security controls field visibility and editability, not the order or visibility of quick action buttons.\n* D: Time zone settings affect date/time display formatting and have no relationship to mobile action bar layout or ordering."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A support team wants a Chatter group where only designated moderators can post updates, but all support agents can read and react to posts, ideal for official announcements. Which Chatter group type satisfies this?", options: [("A", "A private group, since membership requires approval before joining."), ("B", "An unlisted group, since it is hidden from search and requires invitation."), ("C", "A public group, since anyone in the org can see and join it freely."), ("D", "A broadcast-only group, since only group owners/managers can post while members can read and react.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A broadcast-only group restricts posting to owners/managers while allowing all members to read and react, precisely matching the \"official announcements, read-only for most members\" requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: A standard public group allows any member to post freely, which doesn't restrict posting to moderators only as required.\n* B: An unlisted group's defining characteristic is being hidden from search and invitation-only, not restricted posting rights — this doesn't address the posting restriction requirement.\n* A: A private group controls who can join via approval, but doesn't inherently restrict which members can post once they're in, unlike broadcast-only."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A company wants a Chatter feed to automatically show a post whenever an Opportunity's Stage or Amount changes, without any custom automation. Which feature accomplishes this?", options: [("A", "Chatter Feed Tracking, configured on the Opportunity object to track the Stage and Amount fields (up to 20 fields per object), which automatically posts \"Field changed from X to Y\" to the record's feed."), ("B", "A workflow rule that posts a Chatter message on every save."), ("C", "A validation rule that requires a Chatter post before allowing the Stage to change."), ("D", "A custom report scheduled to run whenever Stage changes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Feed Tracking is a native, no-code feature that automatically posts a change entry to a record's Chatter feed whenever a tracked field (up to 20 per object) changes, exactly matching the \"automatic post on field change\" requirement with zero custom automation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: While a workflow rule could theoretically post to Chatter, it requires actual automation setup and isn't the native \"without any custom automation\" mechanism the scenario calls for — Feed Tracking is the purpose-built, zero-automation feature.\n* C: A validation rule can only block or allow a save; it cannot require or generate a Chatter post as part of its logic.\n* D: A scheduled report runs at time intervals and has no mechanism to trigger based on a specific field's real-time value change, nor does it post to Chatter."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Choose 3. A company is deciding which of several requirements can be satisfied using native Salesforce sharing and security features without any custom code. Which three of the following are natively supported, no-code capabilities? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Restricting record visibility using Organization-Wide Defaults combined with role hierarchy."), ("B", "Creating a permission set to grant temporary elevated access to a specific user."), ("C", "Generating a legally binding e-signature workflow natively with zero additional configuration or packages."), ("D", "Configuring field-level security to hide a sensitive field from a specific profile."), ("E", "Automatically translating all custom field labels into 40 languages with zero setup.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: OWD combined with role hierarchy, permission sets for user-specific access, and field-level security are all core, native, no-code Salesforce security features usable out of the box. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Salesforce has no native e-signature capability at all — this always requires an AppExchange package or custom-built solution, not a native feature.\n* E: Translation Workbench requires an admin to configure supported languages and translate labels; it does not translate \"automatically\" with \"zero setup\" as claimed."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "An org-wide default of \"Public Read Only\" is set on the Case object. A support manager wants their team to be able to edit only Cases owned by their own team members, without changing the OWD for the entire org. What sharing tool should be used?", options: [("A", "Grant \"Modify All Data\" to every member of the support team."), ("B", "A criteria-based sharing rule granting Read/Write access to all Cases where a \"Team\" field equals the manager's team, shared with the team's public group."), ("C", "Ask each team member to manually share each Case they need to edit."), ("D", "Change the org-wide default for Case to Public Read/Write for everyone.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A criteria-based sharing rule can grant Read/Write access specifically to records matching the team's criteria, shared only with that team's public group — extending edit access precisely where needed without changing the org-wide baseline for everyone else. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Changing OWD to Public Read/Write grants edit access to literally everyone in the org on every Case, far exceeding the scoped requirement for just one team.\n* A: \"Modify All Data\" grants sweeping, org-wide access to every object and record, dramatically overshooting the narrow, Case-specific requirement.\n* C: Manual sharing for every case is not scalable, is not automatic for new records, and is an inappropriate substitute for an ongoing, rule-based sharing need."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A user's profile grants \"View All\" on the Opportunity object. What is the effect of this permission on record visibility, and how does it differ from simply having a high position in the role hierarchy?", options: [("A", "\"View All\" only shows records owned by direct reports in the role hierarchy, same as a manager role."), ("B", "\"View All\" has no effect unless the org-wide default is also set to Public Read Only."), ("C", "\"View All\" grants read access to every Opportunity record in the org regardless of OWD, sharing rules, or ownership, completely bypassing and ignoring the role hierarchy structure."), ("D", "\"View All\" only applies to records created in the last 30 days.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: \"View All\" on an object is a flat, hierarchy-independent permission that grants read access to every record on that object regardless of OWD or sharing configuration — a fundamentally different, more sweeping mechanism than role-hierarchy-based access. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This describes standard role hierarchy access, not \"View All\" — \"View All\" grants access to everyone's records, not just direct reports.\n* B: \"View All\" functions independently of whatever OWD is set; it overrides OWD and sharing restrictions rather than depending on a specific OWD value.\n* D: There's no time-based restriction on \"View All\" — it applies to all matching records regardless of creation date."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Choose 2. A company needs to design record access so that Account Team members get access to specific related records, not just the Account itself. Which two related object access levels can be individually configured for an Account Team member?", options: [("A", "Access to related Contacts (Read or Edit)."), ("B", "Access to related Opportunities (Read or Edit)."), ("C", "Access to related Chatter groups the account owner has joined."), ("D", "Access to the account owner's personal calendar."), ("E", "Access to unrelated Accounts owned by other users in a different business unit.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Account Teams allow the account owner to grant each team member independent Read or Edit access specifically to the Account's related Contacts and Opportunities (and Cases), in addition to the Account record itself — this is the defining capability of Account Teams. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Account Teams have no mechanism for granting access to Chatter groups; group membership is managed independently through Chatter's own group settings.\n* D: Account Teams control access to Salesforce records related to the Account; they have no connection to a user's personal calendar.\n* E: Account Teams only grant access scoped to that specific Account's related records; they cannot extend access to unrelated Accounts in other business units."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A company's support queue currently has cases piling up because reps must actively search for unassigned cases. Leadership wants any of the 8 support reps to be able to pick up any unassigned case from a shared pool, with ownership transferring cleanly to whoever accepts it. What Salesforce feature and configuration solves this?", options: [("A", "A public group with \"View All\" access to Cases, so all reps can browse and comment on cases owned by others."), ("B", "A criteria-based sharing rule that shares all cases with all 8 reps equally without any ownership change."), ("C", "A Chatter group where all cases are manually posted for reps to claim by commenting \"I'll take this one.\""), ("D", "A Case queue with all 8 reps as members; unassigned cases are routed to the queue, and any member can accept a case, becoming its owner and removing it from the queue.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A queue is purpose-built for this exact scenario — unassigned records are shared with all queue members, and any member can accept a record, cleanly transferring ownership and removing it from the shared pool, which is the standard Salesforce case routing pattern. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Granting \"View All\" lets reps see all cases but does nothing to structure an acceptance/ownership-transfer workflow for unassigned work.\n* C: A manual Chatter-based claiming process is unstructured, doesn't formally transfer ownership, and doesn't scale reliably compared to native queue functionality.\n* B: Sharing cases without any ownership transfer mechanism doesn't solve the core need — reps still wouldn't have a clean way to claim and own a case from the shared pool."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "A retail company wants to display store locations within 25 miles of a customer's address on a Case record to route field technicians efficiently. Which native Salesforce field type and formula approach supports this without code?", options: [("A", "A Geolocation field storing latitude/longitude, combined with the DISTANCE() formula function to calculate proximity."), ("B", "A Roll-Up Summary field that counts the number of nearby stores."), ("C", "A Picklist field manually listing which stores are \"nearby\" for each possible customer address."), ("D", "A Text field storing the address as a string, compared using CONTAINS() to nearby city names.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The Geolocation field type natively stores latitude/longitude coordinates, and the DISTANCE() formula function calculates the distance between two geolocation points, providing exactly the declarative proximity calculation capability needed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Comparing text strings of city names cannot calculate an actual numeric distance in miles between two addresses; it can only do exact or substring text matching.\n* B: A Roll-Up Summary field only aggregates numeric values from child records via a master-detail relationship; it has no capability to calculate geographic distance.\n* C: Manually maintaining a picklist of \"nearby\" stores for every possible customer address is not scalable and would need constant manual updates as customers and stores change."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "Choose 2. A company wants to configure their support Chatter presence for two different needs: (1) a space where all 200 support agents can freely discuss cases informally, and (2) a space visible only to a sensitive internal investigations team that should not even appear in search results. Which two Chatter group type choices correctly match these two needs?", options: [("A", "For need 1, a standard public group, since anyone in the org can freely see and join it for open discussion."), ("B", "For need 2, an unlisted group, since it is hidden from search and requires members to be explicitly invited."), ("C", "For need 1, an unlisted group, since hiding it from search encourages more open discussion."), ("D", "For need 2, a broadcast-only group, since broadcast groups are always hidden from non-members."), ("E", "For need 2, a public group with a locked description field.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A standard public group is ideal for open, freely joinable discussion among all support agents, while an unlisted group specifically provides the hidden-from-search, invitation-only characteristics needed for a sensitive investigations team. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Hiding an open discussion space from search would work against the goal of broad, easy participation by all 200 agents.\n* D: Broadcast-only groups control who can post (only owners/managers), not visibility in search; they are not inherently hidden from non-members the way unlisted groups are.\n* E: A public group is visible and joinable by anyone in the org, which directly contradicts the requirement that the investigations space not even appear in search results; a locked description field has no bearing on group visibility."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A Field Service company needs its 150 field technicians to see only work orders assigned to them, but dispatch supervisors need to see all work orders across all technicians in their district. The Work Order custom object currently has \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" disabled. What must the architect confirm or change to make role hierarchy access work as expected?", options: [("A", "Role hierarchy access only applies to standard objects like Opportunity and Case, never to custom objects, so a sharing rule must be used instead regardless of this setting."), ("B", "The architect must enable \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" on the Work Order custom object, since with it disabled, supervisors above technicians in the role hierarchy will NOT automatically inherit access to their subordinates' work orders."), ("C", "Nothing needs to change; \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" being disabled has no effect on custom objects."), ("D", "The architect must disable OWD entirely on Work Order to allow hierarchy-based access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" is a custom-object-specific setting (defaulted differently than standard objects) that must be enabled for supervisors to automatically inherit access to their subordinates' records through the role hierarchy; with it disabled, that automatic upward access simply does not happen. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This setting has a direct, meaningful effect specifically on custom objects — when disabled, hierarchy-based access does not flow upward at all, contradicting the claim of \"no effect.\"\n* D: OWD and \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" are separate, independent settings; disabling OWD entirely isn't a real configuration path and isn't required to enable hierarchy-based access.\n* A: Role hierarchy access absolutely can apply to custom objects — it's precisely the \"Grant Access Using Hierarchies\" setting (available and configurable on custom objects, unlike some standard objects) that controls this.\n\n# Data Modeling and Management\n\n# Data Modeling and Management"),
        Question(id: "24", question: "A company runs marketing campaigns that promote multiple products, and each product can appear in multiple campaigns. Each campaign-product pairing needs its own discount percentage and start date that vary per pairing. What data model correctly supports this?", options: [("A", "Store all campaign-product pairings and their discounts as comma-separated text in a single long text field on Campaign."), ("B", "Add a lookup field on Product pointing to a single Campaign, allowing only one campaign per product."), ("C", "Create a junction object (e.g., \"Campaign Product\") with two master-detail relationships — one to Campaign and one to Product — where each junction record holds the pairing's own discount percentage and start date fields."), ("D", "Add a multi-select picklist field on Campaign listing all associated Product names.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A junction object with two master-detail relationships is the standard Salesforce pattern for many-to-many relationships, and because each junction record is its own record, it can hold pairing-specific attributes like a unique discount percentage and start date. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: A multi-select picklist can associate multiple products with a campaign, but it cannot store additional attributes (like a discount percentage) that are specific to each individual pairing.\n* B: A single lookup field enforces a one-to-many relationship (one campaign per product), which contradicts the stated many-to-many requirement.\n* A: Storing structured relational data as comma-separated text abandons relational integrity, cannot be reported on cleanly, and cannot enforce data types for the discount or date values."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "An architect is modeling Order Line Items as children of Order, where line items must never exist without a parent order, deleting an order should delete all its line items, and the order needs a roll-up field showing total line item count. Which relationship type must be used?", options: [("A", "A self-relationship, since Order Line Item and Order share similar attributes."), ("B", "A hierarchical relationship, since it is designed for parent-child object modeling."), ("C", "A lookup relationship, since lookups are more flexible for future changes."), ("D", "A master-detail relationship, since it enforces a required parent, cascades delete to children, and is the only relationship type on which roll-up summary fields can be created.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Master-detail relationships are the only relationship type that enforces a required parent (no blank), cascades delete automatically, and supports roll-up summary fields on the parent — satisfying all three stated requirements simultaneously. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: A lookup relationship makes the parent optional, does not cascade delete, and does not support roll-up summary fields, failing all three requirements.\n* B: Hierarchical relationships are a special lookup type available only on the User object; they cannot be created on custom objects like Order Line Item.\n* A: A self-relationship is a lookup from an object to itself (like Account's Parent Account field); it does not apply to a distinct parent-child object pairing like Order and Order Line Item."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is comparing master-detail and lookup relationships before finalizing a data model for a new Invoice/Invoice Line object pair. Which two capabilities exist ONLY with a master-detail relationship, not with lookup?", options: [("A", "Roll-up summary fields on the parent object aggregating child values (SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX)."), ("B", "Cascade delete, where deleting the parent automatically deletes all child records."), ("C", "The ability to create a related list of child records on the parent's page layout."), ("D", "The ability for the child record to have its own Owner field independent of the parent."), ("E", "The ability to filter which records appear in the lookup search dialog.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Roll-up summary fields and cascade delete are both exclusively available on master-detail relationships; lookup relationships support neither of these capabilities natively. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Both master-detail and lookup relationships create a related list of child records on the parent's page layout; this is not exclusive to master-detail.\n* D: Having an independent Owner field is actually a characteristic of lookup relationships, not master-detail — master-detail children inherit ownership from the parent and have no independent Owner field.\n* E: Lookup filters are a capability specifically associated with lookup relationships (to restrict which records appear in the search dialog); this is not a master-detail-exclusive feature."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A custom object already has two master-detail relationships (to Project and to Vendor). The business now wants to add a required, cascade-deleting relationship to a third parent object, Region. What must the architect tell the business?", options: [("A", "This is not possible declaratively; a custom object supports a maximum of 2 master-detail relationships, so an alternative design (e.g., a lookup to Region, or restructuring the model) must be used instead."), ("B", "This is easily configured by simply adding a third master-detail relationship field on the custom object."), ("C", "The third master-detail relationship is possible only after removing the roll-up summary fields from the first two relationships."), ("D", "The third master-detail relationship is possible only if Region is a standard object.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce enforces a hard platform limit of 2 master-detail relationships per custom object, specifically because more than two would create ambiguous ownership and OWD inheritance rules; a third relationship must be modeled as a lookup or via a different structure. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This directly contradicts the documented platform limit of 2 master-detail relationships per custom object; it cannot simply be added.\n* D: The 2-master-detail limit applies to the custom object itself regardless of whether the parent objects are standard or custom; making Region standard does not lift the limit.\n* C: Removing roll-up summary fields has no bearing on the fixed platform limit for the number of master-detail relationship fields a custom object can have."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A company wants to model corporate account hierarchies, where an Account can have a parent company that is also an Account, potentially several levels deep (subsidiary of a subsidiary). What relationship pattern natively supports this?", options: [("A", "A junction object between two Account records for every parent-subsidiary pairing."), ("B", "A self-relationship: a lookup field on Account (the standard \"Parent Account\" field) that points to another Account record, allowing a multi-level corporate hierarchy to be modeled."), ("C", "A master-detail relationship from Account to itself, enabling cascade delete of subsidiaries."), ("D", "A hierarchical relationship field on Account, since hierarchical relationships are designed for any parent-child structure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The standard \"Parent Account\" field is a self-relationship lookup — a lookup field on Account pointing to another Account record — which is exactly the native, built-in mechanism for modeling multi-level corporate hierarchies. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Hierarchical relationships are a special relationship type available only on the User standard object; they cannot be created on Account or other custom/standard objects.\n* C: A master-detail relationship from Account to itself is not a supported configuration for this hierarchy use case, and cascade delete of subsidiaries would be a dangerous, unintended side effect for a corporate hierarchy model.\n* A: A junction object is used to resolve many-to-many relationships between two different objects; a simple self-relationship lookup is the correct, much simpler native pattern for a hierarchical parent-subsidiary structure on the same object."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "An architect needs to store configuration thresholds (e.g., maximum discount percentages per product line) that must be: referenceable directly in validation rule formulas, deployable via change sets without separate data migration, and manageable per record. Which feature should be used instead of a custom object or Custom Setting?", options: [("A", "A Hierarchy Custom Setting, since hierarchy settings can always be referenced directly inside validation rule formulas."), ("B", "A Custom Object, since custom object records provide the most flexibility for storing any kind of data."), ("C", "A Custom Metadata Type, since its records are treated as deployable metadata (travel with change sets/packages) and can be referenced directly in validation rule formulas using the $CustomMetadata merge field, unlike custom object records or standard Custom Settings in formulas."), ("D", "A standard picklist field, since picklist values are inherently deployable.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Custom Metadata Type records are metadata (not data), so they deploy automatically with change sets and packages, and they are the only option among these that can be referenced directly inside validation rule formulas via the $CustomMetadata global merge field. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Custom object records are data, not metadata — they require separate data migration (Data Loader, etc.) and cannot be referenced directly inside a validation rule formula.\n* A: Custom Settings (Hierarchy or List) are not directly referenceable inside validation rule formulas the way Custom Metadata Types are via $CustomMetadata; this is a specific, well-documented limitation of Custom Settings.\n* D: A picklist field defines a fixed value list for a single field on a single object; it cannot model configurable, multi-attribute threshold records like the ones described."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A single Opportunity object must support two very different sales teams: Enterprise (long, multi-stage sales cycles with specific Stage values) and SMB (fast, simplified stages), each needing its own picklist values and its own page layout, while remaining on the same underlying object. What is the correct declarative solution?", options: [("A", "Create a single page layout with all possible Stage values and ask each team to manually ignore irrelevant values."), ("B", "Create two separate custom objects, one for Enterprise Opportunities and one for SMB Opportunities."), ("C", "Use two different Custom Metadata Types to define which Stage values each team should see."), ("D", "Create two Record Types on Opportunity (Enterprise and SMB), each linked to its own Sales Process (controlling which Stage values are available) and its own page layout, then assign each record type to the appropriate team's profiles.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Record Types are specifically designed to control which picklist values are available, which page layout is shown, and which business process (Sales Process) applies — exactly the three things this scenario requires to be different for Enterprise versus SMB while staying on the same Opportunity object. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Splitting into two separate custom objects abandons the standard Opportunity object's built-in sales functionality (forecasting, pipeline reporting) and unnecessarily fragments a process that should stay unified.\n* C: Custom Metadata Types are not the platform mechanism that controls which picklist values or page layout appear to users; that is specifically the role of Record Types and Sales Processes.\n* A: Presenting all possible Stage values to both teams and relying on manual discipline to ignore irrelevant ones defeats the purpose of tailoring the process per team and invites data entry errors."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "A company's Case object currently uses the Master record type only. A new Support Process is created for a \"Technical Escalation\" record type that should show a \"Pending Engineering\" status not available to standard support cases. What must be configured for a Technical Escalation case to actually display this new status value?", options: [("A", "Create the new Support Process including \"Pending Engineering,\" link it to the Technical Escalation record type, and ensure the appropriate profiles have that record type assigned so users can create records with it."), ("B", "Simply add \"Pending Engineering\" to the master Case Status picklist and it will automatically be available on every record type."), ("C", "Rename the Master record type to \"Technical Escalation\" since only one record type can exist per object at a time."), ("D", "Add \"Pending Engineering\" as a new custom field instead of a Status picklist value.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A Support Process defines which Status values are available for a specific Case record type; the new value must be included in a Support Process linked to the Technical Escalation record type, and users need that record type assigned via their profile to actually see and use it. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Simply adding a value to the master picklist does not automatically make it appear for a specific record type — each record type's linked Support Process controls which subset of master values is actually available to that record type's users.\n* C: Multiple record types can and do coexist on a single object simultaneously; renaming the Master record type is not how a new record type is introduced.\n* D: The requirement is specifically about a Status picklist value on Case, which uses the Support Process mechanism; creating an unrelated custom field does not achieve the stated business goal at all."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "Choose 2. An architect is evaluating whether to model a new \"Renewal Term\" concept as fields directly on the Contract object versus as a related child custom object. The business wants: each contract to potentially have several renewal terms over its life, and each renewal term to independently track its own start date, price, and approval status. Which two considerations favor a separate child object over adding fields directly to Contract?", options: [("A", "The one-to-many nature of the relationship (a contract can have several renewal terms over time), which cannot be cleanly represented by a fixed set of fields on the parent."), ("B", "The need for each renewal term to have its own independently trackable lifecycle data (start date, price, approval status) that would require an unbounded, growing number of duplicate field sets on Contract if kept on the parent."), ("C", "The desire to make the Contract object's API name shorter."), ("D", "The fact that Contract is a standard object and therefore cannot have any new custom fields added to it at all."), ("E", "A preference for fewer total objects in the org to simplify the Schema Builder diagram visually.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: When a concept is inherently one-to-many and each instance needs its own independent set of tracked attributes over time, a related child object (rather than a fixed, non-repeating set of fields on the parent) is the correct relational modeling choice — both of these considerations point directly to that conclusion. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: API name length has no bearing on the correct relational structure for representing a one-to-many, independently-tracked concept.\n* D: Standard objects like Contract absolutely can have custom fields added to them; this claim is factually incorrect and irrelevant to the modeling decision anyway.\n* E: Minimizing the number of objects for diagram simplicity is a cosmetic preference that should never override correct relational modeling of genuinely one-to-many, independently lifecycle-tracked data."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A field currently defined as Text is storing free-form city names, but the business now wants to standardize entry to a fixed list of approved cities and enforce consistency going forward. What should the architect confirm before converting this field to a Picklist?", options: [("A", "Converting to Picklist will automatically delete all historical data with no other effect."), ("B", "Existing text values that don't exactly match a defined picklist value will become stale, non-matching entries; if \"Restrict picklist to the values in the value set\" is enabled, these stale values may fail validation on save, so a data cleanup pass should be planned before or after the conversion."), ("C", "Picklist fields cannot be created from an existing Text field under any circumstances; a brand-new field must always be created instead."), ("D", "Converting to Picklist will automatically recalculate all roll-up summary fields that reference the field.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Changing a Text field to Picklist does not delete data, but any existing value that doesn't exactly match a defined picklist value becomes a stale entry, and if the value set is restricted, that stale value can cause validation failures on subsequent saves — making a data cleanup plan essential. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The conversion does not delete historical data; existing values are preserved, though they may become stale/non-matching picklist entries.\n* C: Salesforce does support directly changing a Text field's type to Picklist through the field type change wizard; a brand-new field is not strictly required.\n* D: Roll-up summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no direct relationship to a Text-to-Picklist field type conversion and are not automatically recalculated because of it."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A field must store a large block of user-entered notes, up to 5,000 characters, with no need for bold/italic formatting or embedded images, and must remain usable in Apex text-processing logic. Which field type is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Standard Text field, since it is the simplest field type available."), ("B", "Text Area (Rich), since it supports the largest character capacity."), ("C", "Text Area (Long), since it supports up to 131,072 characters without HTML formatting overhead, comfortably accommodating the 5,000-character need while remaining fully usable in more automation and reporting contexts than Rich Text."), ("D", "A Currency field, since it can store numeric character counts.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Text Area (Long) supports up to 131,072 characters of plain text without the HTML formatting overhead and automation/reporting limitations of Rich Text, making it the right fit for a large notes field with no formatting requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Text Area (Rich) stores HTML-formatted content but has documented limitations — it cannot be referenced in formula fields and cannot be used as a report filter criterion — making it a worse fit when no formatting is actually needed.\n* A: A standard Text field caps out at only 255 characters, far short of the 5,000-character requirement described.\n* D: A Currency field stores monetary numeric values, not free-form text notes; it is entirely the wrong data type for this requirement."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Choose 2. An architect needs a controlling/dependent picklist pair where selecting a \"Product Family\" value filters which \"Product Sub-Type\" values are available. Which two statements about field dependencies are accurate?", options: [("A", "The controlling field must be a single-select picklist or a checkbox field; multi-select picklists cannot serve as controlling fields."), ("B", "The dependent field can be either a single-select picklist or a multi-select picklist."), ("C", "Any field type, including Text and Number fields, can serve as either the controlling or dependent field in a dependency."), ("D", "Field dependencies automatically create a master-detail relationship between the controlling and dependent fields."), ("E", "A dependent field's available values are fixed permanently at creation and can never be changed after the dependency is set up.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Controlling fields are restricted to single-select picklists or checkboxes (multi-select picklists are excluded), while dependent fields can be either single-select or multi-select picklists — both are accurate, documented rules of the field dependency feature. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Text and Number fields cannot serve as controlling or dependent fields in a field dependency; only picklist and checkbox field types are eligible depending on the role.\n* D: Field dependencies are a picklist value-filtering configuration entirely separate from relationship fields; they do not create or imply any master-detail relationship.\n* E: The dependency matrix mapping which dependent values are available for each controlling value can be edited at any time by an admin; it is not permanently fixed at creation."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A multi-select picklist field called \"Preferred Contact Methods\" stores values like \"Email;Phone;SMS\" for each Contact. A formula field needs to check whether \"Email\" is one of the selected values. Which function must be used, and why can't ISPICKVAL() work here?", options: [("A", "CONTAINS() must be used instead, since multi-select picklists are internally stored as Rich Text."), ("B", "ISPICKVAL() works perfectly for multi-select picklists as long as the values are semicolon-delimited."), ("C", "No formula function can evaluate a multi-select picklist field under any circumstances."), ("D", "INCLUDES() must be used, since it is specifically designed to check whether a given value is present within a multi-select picklist's semicolon-delimited string of selected values; ISPICKVAL() only supports single-value picklists.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: INCLUDES() is purpose-built to check for the presence of a specific value within a multi-select picklist's underlying semicolon-delimited string, while ISPICKVAL() is explicitly limited to single-value picklist fields and cannot correctly parse multi-select values. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: ISPICKVAL() does not correctly handle multi-select picklists at all; using it against a multi-select field does not reliably detect individual selected values.\n* A: Multi-select picklists are not stored as Rich Text internally; while CONTAINS() might coincidentally work on the raw string in some cases, INCLUDES() is the correct, purpose-built, documented function for this exact use case.\n* C: Multi-select picklist fields absolutely can be evaluated in formulas using the correct function (INCLUDES()); the claim that no function can evaluate them is false."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A company needs a unique identifier field on a custom object that will be used as the match key for upsert operations from an external ERP system via Data Loader, ensuring no duplicate ERP records are ever created. What field configuration satisfies this?", options: [("A", "A Text field with both the \"External ID\" checkbox and the \"Unique\" checkbox enabled, allowing it to serve as an indexed, duplicate-preventing upsert match key recognized by Data Loader and the API."), ("B", "A Formula field referencing the record's Salesforce Id, since Salesforce Ids are always unique."), ("C", "A Picklist field listing all known ERP record identifiers as static values."), ("D", "A standard Auto Number field, since auto numbers are always guaranteed unique across upserts from any external system.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A Text field marked as both External ID and Unique is indexed for fast lookup and enforces no-duplicate values, making it the correct, standard configuration for an upsert match key tied to an external system's identifier. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Auto Number fields are generated internally by Salesforce sequentially; they cannot be set to match an external system's own ID values during an upsert operation.\n* B: A formula field referencing the Salesforce record Id is read-only and derived after the record already exists in Salesforce; it cannot serve as an externally-supplied match key for identifying whether a record should be inserted or updated during upsert.\n* C: A static picklist of known ERP identifiers cannot scale as new ERP records are created, and picklists are not designed to serve as unique, indexed match keys for upsert operations."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "An architect is deciding whether configurable threshold values used in Apex, Flow, and validation rules should be stored in a Custom Metadata Type or a Hierarchy Custom Setting, given a requirement that certain users should see a different threshold value than the org default. Which is more appropriate, and why?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field configured on the User object."), ("B", "Hierarchy Custom Setting, since it is specifically designed to store values that can be overridden at the org, profile, or user level, with the most specific level taking precedence — exactly matching the need for certain users to see a different value than the org default."), ("C", "Custom Metadata Type, since its records can override values per user in the same way Hierarchy Custom Settings do."), ("D", "A single Text field on the User object populated manually for each user who needs an override.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Hierarchy Custom Settings are purpose-built for exactly this scenario — storing a value that can be set at the org level by default but overridden at the profile or individual user level, with the most specific applicable level automatically winning. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Custom Metadata Type records do not have a native org/profile/user override hierarchy mechanism the way Hierarchy Custom Settings do; that specific override behavior is the defining feature of Hierarchy Custom Settings, not Custom Metadata Types.\n* D: Manually populating a Text field on every user needing an override does not scale, provides no fallback to an org default, and is a much weaker, unmanaged substitute for a native Hierarchy Custom Setting.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate child record values through a master-detail relationship; the User object cannot have a master-detail relationship configured this way, and this doesn't address per-user configuration override needs."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "A company needs to display product inventory data that lives in an external SAP system in real time inside Salesforce, without duplicating or storing that data inside the Salesforce database, while still supporting related lists and record detail pages within the Salesforce UI. What is the correct feature?", options: [("A", "A Data Import Wizard job scheduled to run every hour."), ("B", "A Custom Metadata Type record manually updated by an admin whenever inventory changes."), ("C", "Salesforce Connect, configured with an appropriate adapter (OData or custom), exposing the SAP inventory data as external objects that display in real time in Salesforce UI (including related lists and record detail pages) without storing the data natively."), ("D", "A scheduled Batch Apex job that copies inventory data into a custom object nightly.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce Connect is specifically designed to expose external system data as external objects, displaying it in real time within standard Salesforce UI elements like related lists and record detail pages, without copying or storing the data inside Salesforce. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: A nightly Batch Apex copy job duplicates and stores the data inside Salesforce, directly contradicting the explicit \"without duplicating or storing\" and \"real time\" requirements.\n* A: The Data Import Wizard performs one-time or periodic imports that create actual stored records in Salesforce; it does not provide real-time, non-duplicative access to external data.\n* B: Manually updating a Custom Metadata Type record whenever inventory changes is not real-time, not automated, and not a genuine integration mechanism for live external data."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A data migration team needs to import 300,000 new Contact records from a legacy CRM into Salesforce, matching on an external system ID to avoid creating duplicates for contacts that were already partially migrated in a previous batch. Which tool and operation should be used?", options: [("A", "The Data Import Wizard, since it supports Contacts and any record volume."), ("B", "A Roll-Up Summary field configured to detect and merge duplicate contacts automatically."), ("C", "Manually re-entering all 300,000 contacts through the Salesforce UI to ensure accuracy."), ("D", "Data Loader, performing an upsert operation using the previously configured External ID field as the match key, since Data Loader supports large volumes (up to 5 million records) and upsert prevents duplicate creation for already-migrated contacts.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Data Loader supports up to 5 million records (well above the 300,000 needed) and its upsert operation, using the External ID field as the match key, correctly updates already-migrated contacts while inserting genuinely new ones, avoiding duplicates. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The Data Import Wizard is capped at 50,000 records per import, far short of the 300,000-record volume required for this migration.\n* C: Manually re-entering 300,000 records through the UI is operationally infeasible and introduces massive risk of human error at this scale.\n* B: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they have no capability to detect, merge, or prevent duplicate records during a data import."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "Choose 2. A company wants robust duplicate prevention when sales reps manually create new Leads, alerting them if a similar Lead or Contact already exists based on Email and Company Name, without blocking the save outright (since sometimes it's a legitimate new record). Which two native Salesforce features must be configured together to achieve this?", options: [("A", "A Matching Rule defining that Email and Company Name (or similar fields) constitute a potential match between Leads and/or Contacts."), ("B", "A Duplicate Rule that references the matching rule and is configured with an \"Alert\" action (rather than \"Block\") so users are warned but not prevented from saving."), ("C", "A Roll-Up Summary field that counts the number of similar Leads."), ("D", "A validation rule that always blocks any Lead creation if any similar record exists in the org."), ("E", "A Custom Metadata Type record listing every duplicate Lead pair manually.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Matching Rules define what constitutes a potential duplicate match, and Duplicate Rules use that matching logic to determine the resulting action — configuring the Duplicate Rule with an \"Alert\" action (not \"Block\") delivers exactly the warn-but-don't-block behavior requested. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they have no duplicate detection or alerting capability whatsoever.\n* D: A validation rule set to always block would prevent the save entirely, directly contradicting the requirement that legitimate new records not be blocked — this describes a \"Block\" duplicate rule action, not what was asked for.\n* E: Manually maintaining a list of every duplicate pair does not scale and is not how the native, automated Matching Rule and Duplicate Rule framework operates."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "An architect needs to understand the full entity-relationship structure of an unfamiliar org with 40 custom objects and dozens of interconnected lookup and master-detail relationships before proposing changes. What tool is best suited for this discovery task, and what is a key limitation to keep in mind?", options: [("A", "Schema Builder, which visually renders all objects and their relationships on one canvas for easy comprehension; however, it cannot be used to delete fields/objects, manage field dependencies, or configure validation rules — those tasks still require the individual object setup pages."), ("B", "The Data Import Wizard, which displays a full visual map of all object relationships before any import."), ("C", "The Recycle Bin, since it lists all recently deleted objects and their relationships."), ("D", "Report Builder, since custom report types visually diagram all object relationships.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Schema Builder is specifically designed to visually render all objects and their relationship connections on a single interactive canvas, making it ideal for understanding a complex, unfamiliar data model quickly, though it explicitly cannot perform deletions, dependency management, or validation rule configuration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: The Data Import Wizard is a data import tool with no relationship visualization or diagramming capability at all.\n* D: Report Builder is used to construct and run reports; while custom report types define object relationship chains for reporting purposes, it does not provide a visual entity-relationship diagram of the whole org's data model.\n* C: The Recycle Bin stores recently deleted records and metadata for possible restoration; it has no relationship-mapping or diagramming function."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "A junction object has two master-detail relationships: one to Account (OWD: Private) and one to Program (OWD: Public Read Only). A user has access to the related Program record but not the related Account record. Can this user access the junction record, and why?", options: [("A", "Access to junction records is entirely independent of both parent OWDs and is controlled solely by the junction object's own separate OWD setting."), ("B", "Yes, because junction record access follows the more permissive of the two parent OWDs; since the user has access via the Public Read Only Program parent, they can access the junction record even without direct Account access."), ("C", "No, junction objects always inherit only the stricter of the two parent OWDs for security reasons."), ("D", "No, the user needs access to both parent records simultaneously to see the junction record.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: When a junction object has two master-detail relationships to different parents with different OWDs, a user's access to the junction record follows the more permissive of the two parents' access levels — access via the Public Read Only Program parent is sufficient even without direct Account access. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Junction record access does not require simultaneous access to both parents; access through either parent's more permissive path is sufficient.\n* C: This describes the opposite of the actual platform behavior — the more permissive parent access applies, not the stricter one.\n* A: Junction objects with master-detail relationships do not have an independent OWD of their own separate from their parents — access is derived from the parent relationships, not a standalone junction-level setting."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "A Contact lookup field on a custom Service Request object should only allow selection of Contacts belonging to the same Account already selected on the Service Request, and should completely block saving if a user tries to override and pick a mismatched Contact. What lookup filter configuration is required?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field restricting which Contacts are related to the Account."), ("B", "An optional lookup filter, since optional filters still show only matching records by default while allowing convenient overrides when truly needed."), ("C", "A required lookup filter, filtering the Contact lookup so that Contact.AccountId equals the Service Request's selected Account; required filters block the save entirely if the selected value doesn't match the filter criteria, unlike optional filters which merely default to matching records but still permit an override."), ("D", "A validation rule is the only way to filter which records appear in a lookup's search dialog.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A required lookup filter both narrows the search results to matching records and actively blocks saving if a user attempts to select or force a non-matching value, exactly satisfying the \"completely block\" requirement described. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: An optional lookup filter only defaults to showing matching records in the search dialog but still allows the user to override and search for/select a non-matching record, which contradicts the explicit requirement to block that override entirely.\n* D: While a validation rule could theoretically enforce a similar constraint on save, it does not filter which records appear in the lookup search dialog itself — that filtering behavior is specifically the role of a lookup filter, not a validation rule.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no capability to filter or restrict which records appear in an unrelated lookup field's search dialog."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Choose 2. An architect must decide the field type for a new attribute capturing \"Signed Contract Date\" versus \"Exact Timestamp of Digital Signature Event\" on a Contract object, where the signature timestamp must support precise SLA breach calculations down to the minute. Which two statements correctly describe the appropriate field type choices and their formula implications?", options: [("A", "\"Signed Contract Date\" should be a Date field, since only the calendar day matters and no time-of-day precision is needed for this attribute."), ("B", "\"Exact Timestamp of Digital Signature Event\" should be a Date/Time field, since it stores both date and time (in UTC, displayed in the user's time zone) and supports minute-level precision needed for SLA breach calculations."), ("C", "Both fields should be Date/Time fields, since Date fields are being deprecated by Salesforce."), ("D", "Subtracting two Date fields is not supported in Salesforce formulas and always requires a custom Apex method."), ("E", "Both fields should use the Currency data type to allow precise mathematical operations.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A Date field is the correct, simpler choice when only the calendar day matters, while a Date/Time field is required when time-of-day precision (down to the minute) is needed for calculations like SLA breach tracking — matching the field type to the actual precision requirement of each attribute. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Salesforce is not deprecating the Date field type; Date fields remain fully supported and are the correct choice whenever time-of-day precision is unnecessary.\n* D: Salesforce formulas natively support direct subtraction of two Date fields (Date2 - Date1), returning the number of days as a number, with no custom Apex required.\n* E: Currency is a monetary data type meant for financial amounts; it is entirely inappropriate for storing or calculating date or timestamp values.\n\n# Business Logic and Process Automation\n\n# Business Logic and Process Automation"),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A formula field must display \"High Value\" when Opportunity Amount exceeds $100,000, \"Medium Value\" between $50,000 and $100,000, and \"Standard\" otherwise. Which function structure is most appropriate, and why not CASE()?", options: [("A", "CASE(), since it is always preferred over IF() for any formula involving more than one possible outcome."), ("B", "A Roll-Up Summary field, since it can categorize Amount into text buckets natively."), ("C", "TEXT(), since it converts any number into a categorized label automatically."), ("D", "Nested IF() statements, since CASE() only performs exact-match equality comparisons against discrete values and cannot evaluate greater-than/less-than numeric range conditions like this scenario requires.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: CASE() is restricted to exact-match comparisons against a fixed set of discrete values, so it cannot express greater-than/less-than range logic; nested IF() statements correctly handle the range-based conditions needed here. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: CASE() is not universally preferred — it is specifically weaker than IF() precisely when range-based (rather than exact-match) conditions are required, as in this scenario.\n* B: Roll-Up Summary fields only aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they have no categorization or labeling capability for a single record's own field.\n* C: TEXT() simply converts a value to its text representation; it has no built-in logic to categorize a number into business-defined labels like \"High Value\" or \"Medium Value.\""),
        Question(id: "47", question: "A formula field on Contact must display the related Account's Industry field value directly on the Contact record. What is the correct formula syntax pattern, and what is the relevant traversal limit to keep in mind?", options: [("A", "Account.Industry, using the standard relationship name; formulas can traverse up to 5 levels deep and reference up to 10 unique relationships total."), ("B", "INCLUDES(Account, Industry), since cross-object references require the INCLUDES() function."), ("C", "Account__r.Industry, since all cross-object references always require the __r suffix regardless of relationship type."), ("D", "A Roll-Up Summary field is required instead, since formulas cannot reference parent object fields.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: For a standard relationship like Contact-to-Account, the syntax uses the relationship name directly (Account.Industry), and formulas support traversing up to 5 levels deep while referencing up to 10 unique relationships in total. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: The __r suffix is specifically required for custom lookup/master-detail relationships (e.g., Custom_Object__r.Field__c), not for standard relationships like Account on Contact, which uses the relationship name directly.\n* B: INCLUDES() is a function specifically for checking values within multi-select picklists; it has no role in cross-object field references.\n* D: Formula fields absolutely can reference parent object fields via cross-object formulas; a Roll-Up Summary field is unrelated and would not display a text field like Industry from the parent anyway (roll-ups only aggregate numeric values from children, not display parent text fields)."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "A validation rule needs to require a business justification text field whenever an Opportunity's discount exceeds 25%, but should not block saves when the discount is 25% or below, even if the justification field happens to be blank. Which formula correctly implements this conditional requirement?", options: [("A", "ISBLANK(Justification__c)"), ("B", "AND(Discount__c > 25, ISBLANK(Justification__c))"), ("C", "OR(Discount__c > 25, ISBLANK(Justification__c))"), ("D", "Discount__c > 25")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The validation rule should only block the save (return TRUE, triggering an error) when BOTH conditions are true simultaneously — discount exceeds 25% AND the justification is blank — which is exactly what AND() expresses; a save with discount at or below 25% never blocks regardless of the justification field. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: OR() would block the save whenever EITHER condition is true, meaning any blank justification would block the save even at a 10% discount, which is far broader than the actual conditional requirement.\n* A: This blocks every save with a blank justification regardless of discount percentage, ignoring the 25% threshold condition entirely.\n* D: This blocks every save where discount exceeds 25%, regardless of whether justification is filled in or not, ignoring the actual field-blank condition that should determine whether to block."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "A validation rule must ensure a Text field named \"Reference Code\" is either completely empty OR, if filled in, must be exactly 8 characters long. Which formula meets this requirement by blocking only the invalid \"wrong length\" case?", options: [("A", "ISBLANK(Reference_Code__c)"), ("B", "LEN(Reference_Code__c) <> 8"), ("C", "AND(NOT(ISBLANK(Reference_Code__c)), LEN(Reference_Code__c) <> 8)"), ("D", "OR(ISBLANK(Reference_Code__c), LEN(Reference_Code__c) = 8)")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The validation rule must only block saves where the field is filled in AND has the wrong length; wrapping the length check with a check that the field is not blank ensures a truly blank field is always allowed to save, correctly implementing the \"empty OR exactly 8 characters\" requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This blocks the save any time the field's length isn't 8, including when the field is completely blank (length 0), which incorrectly prevents legitimately empty values from saving.\n* A: This blocks every save whenever the field is blank, which is the opposite of the intended behavior — blank should be explicitly allowed.\n* D: This formula describes the ALLOWED condition, not the blocking condition; a validation rule formula must evaluate to TRUE for the invalid case it should block, not for the valid case it should allow."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "Choose 2. An architect is writing a validation rule to enforce that a Contact's Email field, when populated, must belong to the company's own domain (\"@acmecorp.com\"), while still allowing the Email field to be left blank. Which two formula elements are needed together to correctly express this?", options: [("A", "NOT(ISBLANK(Email)) to check that the field has been populated before evaluating its content."), ("B", "NOT(CONTAINS(Email, \"@acmecorp.com\")) to detect when a populated email does not contain the required domain."), ("C", "ISPICKVAL(Email, \"@acmecorp.com\") to check the email domain value."), ("D", "A Roll-Up Summary field aggregating the Email field across child records."), ("E", "TEXT(Email) to convert the email address into a checkable numeric value.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Combining a not-blank check with a not-contains check for the required domain (joined with AND) correctly blocks only the case where Email is populated but lacks the proper domain, while leaving a blank Email field unblocked. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: ISPICKVAL() is exclusively for evaluating picklist field values; Email is a text-type field, so ISPICKVAL() is not applicable here.\n* D: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they cannot evaluate or validate a text field's content on the same record.\n* E: TEXT() converts non-text values (like picklists or numbers) into text; Email is already a text field, so wrapping it in TEXT() serves no purpose and does not create a \"numeric value.\""),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A Roll-Up Summary field on Account needs to show the total dollar Amount of all related Opportunities, but only those in Closed Won stage, excluding all other stages. What roll-up summary configuration achieves this?", options: [("A", "A roll-up summary cannot filter by Stage; a separate custom object must be created instead."), ("B", "A MAX roll-up summary on the Amount field, since MAX inherently reflects only the highest-value closed deal."), ("C", "A COUNT roll-up summary with no filter criteria, since counting automatically excludes non-Closed-Won records."), ("D", "A SUM roll-up summary on the Amount field, with filter criteria specifying Stage equals Closed Won, so only matching child records contribute to the total.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A SUM roll-up summary field supports filter criteria, and setting the filter to Stage equals Closed Won ensures only matching child Opportunity records contribute their Amount to the aggregated total on the parent Account. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: COUNT tallies the number of matching records, not their dollar Amount; without the correct filter and aggregate type, it does not produce a total dollar sum of Closed Won deals.\n* B: MAX returns only the single highest Amount value among matching records, not the total sum of all Closed Won Amounts, which is a different aggregate entirely.\n* A: Roll-up summary fields explicitly support filter criteria (such as Stage equals a specific value); this is a standard, native capability and does not require a separate custom object."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "An architect wants a roll-up-style aggregation between two objects connected only by a lookup relationship (not master-detail), because master-detail isn't feasible due to other constraints. What are the implications for achieving aggregation in this scenario?", options: [("A", "True roll-up summary fields are not supported on lookup relationships; the team must instead use a workaround such as a Flow or Apex trigger that recalculates and stores an aggregate value on the parent whenever child records change."), ("B", "Roll-up summary fields work identically on lookup relationships as on master-detail; no special workaround is needed."), ("C", "A validation rule can substitute for a roll-up summary field in this case."), ("D", "The lookup relationship must first be manually converted to a master-detail relationship, which is always possible with zero data or configuration impact.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Native roll-up summary fields are exclusively available on master-detail relationships; when only a lookup relationship exists, achieving similar aggregation requires an alternative mechanism like a Flow (using Get Records and an aggregate calculation) or an Apex trigger that manually calculates and stores the aggregate value on the parent. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is factually incorrect — roll-up summary fields are a master-detail-exclusive capability and simply do not function on lookup relationships.\n* D: Converting a lookup to a master-detail relationship is possible in some cases but is not always feasible or impact-free — it can have significant implications for existing sharing, ownership, and data, and the scenario states this conversion isn't feasible due to other constraints.\n* C: A validation rule can only block or allow a save based on a formula; it has no capability to aggregate or sum values across multiple child records."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "Choose 2. A roll-up summary field on Account named \"Total Open Pipeline\" sums the Amount of related Opportunities where Stage is not in a closed status. Which two facts about roll-up summary fields are relevant considerations when designing this field?", options: [("A", "Roll-up summary fields are only available on the master object side of a master-detail relationship; Account would need to be the master and Opportunity the detail for this to work declaratively via a roll-up (in practice, Opportunity-to-Account is a special standard lookup with its own roll-up support)."), ("B", "Roll-up summary fields recalculate automatically whenever a contributing child record is created, updated, or deleted, keeping the aggregate continuously accurate without manual intervention."), ("C", "Roll-up summary fields can aggregate Rich Text Area fields directly."), ("D", "Roll-up summary fields require a corresponding Apex batch job to run nightly in order to update their values."), ("E", "Roll-up summary fields can perform SUM, COUNT, MIN, and MAX aggregations, optionally filtered by criteria on the child records.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Roll-up summary fields automatically and immediately recalculate whenever a contributing child record changes (no manual or scheduled job required), and they support SUM, COUNT, MIN, and MAX aggregate types, optionally filtered by criteria on the child object's fields. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: While roll-up summaries are generally master-detail-exclusive, this option's phrasing about which side must be master introduces confusion versus known aggregation support; the key correct facts about roll-up behavior are captured in B and E, which are unambiguously accurate.\n* C: Roll-up summary fields cannot aggregate Rich Text Area fields; they operate on numeric, currency, or date field types on the child records being aggregated.\n* D: Roll-up summary fields update automatically and immediately in real time upon child record changes; no batch job or nightly process is required for their recalculation."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A procurement approval process requires that purchase requests under $5,000 be auto-approved without any human review, while requests at or above $5,000 require a manager's approval. How should the approval process entry criteria and behavior be configured?", options: [("A", "Do not use entry criteria at all; instead configure every request to require manager approval unconditionally."), ("B", "Set entry criteria to Amount__c >= 5000, so that any request not meeting this criteria (under $5,000) is automatically approved with no human intervention, since records not meeting entry criteria are auto-approved by default."), ("C", "Set entry criteria to Amount__c < 5000, requiring approval for anything below the threshold."), ("D", "Use a validation rule to block all requests under $5,000 from being submitted at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Setting entry criteria to Amount__c >= 5000 means only requests meeting or exceeding the threshold actually enter the approval process for manager review; requests that don't meet the entry criteria (i.e., under $5,000) are automatically approved by default platform behavior, exactly matching the requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This inverts the logic — it would route the low-value under-$5,000 requests into the approval process for manager review while auto-approving the high-value $5,000+ requests, the opposite of the stated business need.\n* A: Requiring unconditional approval for every request eliminates the auto-approval path entirely for low-value requests, contradicting the explicit requirement that sub-$5,000 requests skip human review.\n* D: A validation rule that blocks all under-$5,000 requests would prevent legitimate low-value requests from being submitted at all, rather than auto-approving them as required."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "An approval process step has three assigned approvers, and the business requires that the step advance to \"approved\" as soon as any single one of them approves it, without waiting for the other two to respond. Which approver response setting achieves this?", options: [("A", "\"Require unanimous approval from all selected approvers,\" since unanimous approval processes the fastest response first."), ("B", "Assign only one approver to the step, since only single-approver steps can ever respond quickly."), ("C", "\"Approve or reject based on the first response,\" since this setting advances the step immediately based on whichever assigned approver responds first, regardless of the other approvers' pending status."), ("D", "Use a scheduled path to automatically approve after 24 hours regardless of any approver's response.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The \"Approve or reject based on the first response\" setting is specifically designed so the step's outcome (approved or rejected) is determined immediately by whichever of the multiple assigned approvers responds first, without waiting on the remaining approvers. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Unanimous approval requires ALL assigned approvers to approve before the step advances; it is the opposite behavior of advancing on the very first response.\n* B: The scenario explicitly requires three approvers to remain assigned to the step; reducing to a single approver doesn't satisfy the business requirement of having three people who can potentially respond, it just eliminates the multi-approver aspect.\n* D: A time-based scheduled path is unrelated to human approver response behavior and does not represent \"advancing based on first response\"; it introduces an unrelated automatic timeout mechanism instead."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "A business wants that when a discount approval request is rejected, the record is automatically unlocked for editing and a Status field is set to \"Rejected\" so the submitter can revise and resubmit. Which two configuration areas of the approval process must be set up together to achieve this? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A Field Update action in the Final Rejection Actions section setting Status__c to \"Rejected.\""), ("B", "A Field Update (or unlock) action in the Final Rejection Actions section that unlocks the record for editing."), ("C", "A Field Update action in the Initial Submission Actions section setting Status__c to \"Rejected.\""), ("D", "An entry criteria formula that prevents any record from ever being rejected."), ("E", "A Roll-Up Summary field that recalculates the Status field automatically.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Both the Status field update and the record unlock action must be configured specifically within the Final Rejection Actions section of the approval process, since that is the section that executes precisely when a rejection becomes final — exactly matching the timing described in the requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Initial Submission Actions execute immediately upon submission, before any approver has acted, so setting Status to \"Rejected\" there would incorrectly mark every submitted record as rejected from the start, regardless of the actual outcome.\n* D: Entry criteria determine which records enter the approval process at all; they have no relationship to what happens after a rejection occurs, and preventing all rejections contradicts the scenario's premise that rejections can and do happen.\n* E: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they have no mechanism to set a Status field value based on an approval outcome."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "A company needs an approval process step where the approver is dynamically determined by looking up a specific user referenced in a field on the record itself (e.g., the record's \"Account Manager\" lookup field), rather than a fixed named individual or the submitter's manager. Which approver type supports this?", options: [("A", "Queue, since queues can dynamically resolve to a single field-referenced user."), ("B", "Specific user, since specific users can be dynamically swapped based on field values at runtime."), ("C", "Manager field, since it always resolves to whichever user is referenced by any lookup field on the record."), ("D", "Related User, since this approver type specifically resolves to the user referenced by a specified lookup field on the record itself (such as Account Manager), rather than the submitter's own manager or a hardcoded named user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The \"Related User\" approver type is specifically designed to resolve to whichever user is referenced by a specified lookup field on the record (such as an Account Manager field), making it the correct choice for dynamically determined, field-based approver assignment. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: The \"Manager field\" approver type specifically resolves to the submitting user's own manager in the user role hierarchy, not an arbitrary lookup field referencing any user on the record.\n* A: A Queue approver type routes the approval to any member of a specified queue of users; it does not dynamically resolve to a single user referenced by a record field.\n* B: \"Specific user\" approver types are hardcoded to one or more named individuals at configuration time; they do not dynamically change based on a field value on the record at runtime."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "A time-sensitive approval process needs the submitting user's manager to still be able to approve the request even if that manager is on vacation, by routing the approval task to a designated backup person as well. What feature and setting combination enables this?", options: [("A", "Each user configures a Delegated Approver in their personal user settings, and the approval process step must have \"Allow delegation\" enabled so the delegated approver also receives approval tasks alongside the primary approver."), ("B", "The manager's user record must be deactivated while on vacation to force automatic reassignment."), ("C", "A second, entirely separate approval process must be created and run in parallel."), ("D", "The submitting user must manually reassign the approval request to a different user after submission.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Delegated approvers are configured individually in each user's personal settings, and the approval process step must explicitly enable delegation so that the delegated approver receives approval tasks in addition to the primary manager, exactly supporting coverage during absences like vacation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Manual reassignment after submission requires human intervention every time and isn't the pre-configured, proactive delegation mechanism the scenario is asking about.\n* C: Creating an entirely separate parallel approval process is unnecessarily complex and is not how the native delegated approver feature is designed to work.\n* B: Deactivating a user record removes their system access entirely and has significant unrelated consequences; it is not the correct or intended mechanism for temporary approval coverage during a vacation."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "A business requires that when a record is submitted for approval, it becomes locked from editing by regular users, but the currently assigned approver and system administrators must still be able to edit it while it's pending. What approval process behavior achieves this automatically?", options: [("A", "A validation rule blocking edits by anyone whatsoever while a record is pending approval, with zero exceptions for approvers or admins."), ("B", "Record locking configured as an Initial Submission Action, which automatically restricts editing to only administrators, the current step's approver, and users with \"Modify All\" on the object, while blocking regular users from editing."), ("C", "A Roll-Up Summary field that prevents child record edits while the parent is pending."), ("D", "Removing Edit permission from every user's profile on the object entirely and permanently.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Record locking, configured as an Initial Submission Action in the approval process, is the native platform behavior that automatically restricts editing to administrators, the current approver, and users with \"Modify All,\" while blocking regular users — exactly matching the described requirement with no extra configuration needed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A validation rule that blocks absolutely everyone, including approvers and admins, would prevent the very people who need to review or manage the pending request from being able to act on it, which fails the requirement's exceptions.\n* D: Permanently removing Edit permission from every user's profile disables editing for that object at all times, not just while a specific record is pending approval, which is a much broader and permanent change than intended.\n* C: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values; they have no mechanism to lock or restrict editing on any record based on approval status."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A record-triggered Flow updates a field on the same record that triggered it, then a second automation on the same object also fires based on that same field change, and the business needs to know how many times Flow's update-triggered re-evaluation can recursively cascade before Salesforce intervenes. What governs this?", options: [("A", "Recursion limits only apply to Apex triggers, never to Flow, so Flow can loop indefinitely without any platform safeguard."), ("B", "The recursion limit is determined solely by the org's configured API request limit per 24-hour period."), ("C", "Salesforce enforces a maximum of 5 update-to-the-same-record recursion cycles within a single transaction context for record-triggered Flows (via a savepoint/rollback safeguard) to prevent infinite loops from crashing the transaction."), ("D", "Flow has no protection against infinite recursion; an org must always manually track a static list to prevent runaway loops.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce enforces a documented recursion safeguard that limits record-triggered Flow updates to the same record to a maximum before-save/after-save recursive depth (commonly cited as 5 cycles) within a single transaction, specifically to prevent an infinite update loop from crashing the transaction. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: This is inaccurate — Salesforce does have a built-in recursion safeguard for record-triggered Flows; manually tracking a static list is a best-practice technique used within Flow/Apex design, not the platform's own enforced ceiling.\n* A: Recursion limits are not exclusive to Apex triggers; record-triggered Flows have their own documented recursion protections precisely because Flow-driven update loops are a known risk.\n* B: The API request limit governs external API call volume over 24 hours; it has no relationship to same-transaction recursive Flow update cycles, which are governed by a separate, transaction-scoped safeguard."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A record-triggered Flow performs a DML update inside a loop iterating over 500 related child records, one record at a time inside the loop. What automation design principle is being violated, and what is the correct fix?", options: [("A", "Nothing is wrong; Flow automatically bulkifies any DML placed inside a loop with no design changes needed."), ("B", "The fix is to convert the record-triggered Flow into a Process Builder process, which is immune to this issue."), ("C", "The fix is to add a Pause element inside the loop to slow down the DML operations."), ("D", "The Flow violates bulkification best practice by performing DML inside the loop; the fix is to collect the records to update into a collection variable inside the loop, then perform a single DML \"Update Records\" element outside the loop against the whole collection.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Performing DML inside a loop risks hitting the governor limit on total DML statements per transaction (100); the correct bulkification pattern is to accumulate records into a collection variable during the loop and perform a single DML operation against the entire collection after the loop completes. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Flow does not automatically bulkify DML placed inside a loop — each DML element execution inside a loop counts as a separate DML statement, and this is a well-documented anti-pattern that developers/admins must explicitly avoid.\n* B: Process Builder (now legacy/retired for new builds) has the same underlying DML-in-loop risk as Flow; switching tools does not resolve a fundamentally poor automation design pattern.\n* C: Adding a Pause element does not reduce the number of DML statements being executed; it only introduces execution delay and does not address the governor limit risk at all."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "Choose 2. An org has a before-save record-triggered Flow, an after-save record-triggered Flow, and an Apex trigger all configured on the Opportunity object. Which two statements correctly describe the order-of-execution relationship among these automations?", options: [("A", "Before-save Flow logic (Fast Field Updates) generally executes before after-save automations like after-save Flows and most Apex trigger logic within the same save operation, since it operates in the same phase as before-triggers."), ("B", "After-save record-triggered Flows and Apex \"after\" trigger logic both execute after the record has been committed to the database, in a well-defined but interleaved order governed by Salesforce's documented order of execution."), ("C", "Apex triggers always execute completely before any Flow of any kind, regardless of before-save or after-save configuration."), ("D", "Before-save Flows can only run after all Apex before-triggers on the object have already completed."), ("E", "Roll-up summary field recalculations happen before any before-save Flow update in all cases.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Before-save (fast field update) Flow logic runs early in the same phase as before-triggers, ahead of after-save automations, and both after-save Flows and Apex after-trigger logic run later in the well-documented, interleaved order-of-execution sequence — both statements accurately reflect Salesforce's documented order of execution. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This is incorrect — before-save Flow updates actually execute in the same early phase as before-triggers, not strictly after all Apex of any kind; the platform's order of execution interleaves these based on defined save phases, not a blanket \"Apex always first\" rule.\n* D: Before-save Flows do not wait for all before-triggers to fully complete first; both execute within the same before-save phase according to Salesforce's documented, more nuanced ordering.\n* E: Roll-up summary recalculation timing is tied to the actual DML changes on child records within the transaction's order of execution, not universally guaranteed to precede every before-save Flow update in all cases."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "A validation rule and a before-save record-triggered Flow both act on the Opportunity object's Amount field during a save. In what relative sequence do these two types of automation execute, and why does this matter for design?", options: [("A", "Before-save Flow field updates execute before validation rules are evaluated, meaning a before-save Flow can adjust a field's value and that adjusted value is what the validation rule then checks against — an important sequencing consideration when designing both together."), ("B", "Validation rules only apply to manual UI saves and are automatically skipped for any record touched by a Flow."), ("C", "Validation rules and before-save Flows always execute simultaneously and cannot influence each other's evaluated values."), ("D", "Validation rules always run after all Flow automations, including after-save Flows, so any Flow-set value is guaranteed final before validation ever checks it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Before-save Flow field updates execute earlier in the order of execution than validation rules, so if a before-save Flow changes a field's value, the validation rule evaluates against that already-updated value — a critical design consideration to avoid unexpected validation behavior. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Validation rules run before after-save Flow automations, not after all Flow automation types; after-save Flows execute later, following record commit, well after validation has already passed.\n* C: These automations do not run simultaneously in an unordered way; Salesforce's order of execution defines a specific, deterministic sequence where before-save Flow updates precede validation rule evaluation.\n* B: Validation rules apply to saves regardless of whether they originate from the UI, API, or Flow-triggered updates; they are not selectively skipped based on Flow involvement."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A record-triggered Flow on Case needs to send an outbound callout to an external shipping API and must not fail the entire save transaction if that callout errors, while still logging the failure for review. What Flow design pattern correctly achieves this?", options: [("A", "Place the callout action inside the main after-save Flow path directly with no fault path configured, since after-save Flows automatically retry failed callouts."), ("B", "Configure the callout as part of an asynchronous path (e.g., using a Platform Event or an asynchronous after-save path scheduled element) with a defined Fault Path attached to the callout action to catch errors and log them (e.g., writing to a custom object) without rolling back the original triggering transaction."), ("C", "Use a Roll-Up Summary field to track callout failures automatically."), ("D", "Add the callout directly inside a before-save Flow, since before-save Flows can safely make callouts without any transactional risk.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Callouts must be handled asynchronously (never in a before-save context) and a properly attached Fault Path on the callout action lets errors be caught and logged separately without causing the original triggering save transaction to roll back. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Without a defined Fault Path, an unhandled callout error can cause the Flow interview to fail, and there's no native \"automatic retry\" guarantee being described; proper fault handling requires explicit configuration.\n* D: Before-save Flows explicitly cannot perform callouts at all — callouts are not supported in the before-save context, this is a hard platform restriction.\n* C: A Roll-Up Summary field aggregates numeric values from master-detail children; it has no ability to track or log callout failures or execute any error-handling logic."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "An admin needs to choose between Process Builder and Flow Builder for a new automation requirement in a current Salesforce org. What guidance applies?", options: [("A", "Workflow Rules are the newest automation tool and should be used instead of both Process Builder and Flow Builder."), ("B", "Process Builder and Flow Builder are functionally identical, so the choice is purely aesthetic preference."), ("C", "Process Builder (and Workflow Rules) are retired for creating new automation as of specific retirement milestones; Salesforce recommends Flow Builder as the sole forward-looking declarative automation tool for all new automation, since it offers a superset of both tools' capabilities."), ("D", "Process Builder is the recommended tool for all new automation going forward, since it has a simpler visual interface than Flow Builder.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce has retired Process Builder and Workflow Rules for building new automation, positioning Flow Builder as the single, forward-looking declarative automation tool since it provides a superset of the retired tools' capabilities plus significantly more power (loops, subflows, complex logic, scheduled paths). Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: This directly contradicts Salesforce's official direction — Process Builder is being retired for new automation builds, not recommended as the primary go-forward tool.\n* B: The tools are not functionally identical; Flow Builder offers substantially more capability (loops, complex branching, subflows, scheduled/asynchronous paths) that Process Builder never supported.\n* A: Workflow Rules are the oldest of the three automation tools, not the newest, and are also retired for new automation builds in favor of Flow Builder."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "Choose 2. An architect is deciding whether a business requirement can be met by a Screen Flow versus a record-triggered (autolaunched) Flow. The requirement: when a rep manually clicks a button on an Opportunity, a guided multi-step wizard should collect additional information from the rep before creating related records. Which two facts correctly justify choosing a Screen Flow?", options: [("A", "Screen Flows can present multiple interactive screens for a user to view and input data across a guided, multi-step wizard experience."), ("B", "Screen Flows can be launched from a Lightning page, a quick action/button, or a Lightning App Page, matching the \"rep manually clicks a button\" trigger requirement."), ("C", "Record-triggered Flows can prompt the user with a Screen element to collect input mid-automation."), ("D", "Screen Flows always run entirely in the background with no user interaction required, similar to autolaunched Flows."), ("E", "Only record-triggered Flows are capable of creating related records; Screen Flows cannot create records.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Screen Flows are specifically designed to present interactive, multi-step screens for user input, and they can be launched via a quick action, button, or Lightning page — both facts directly justify choosing a Screen Flow for a manually-triggered, guided data-collection wizard. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Record-triggered (autolaunched) Flows run entirely in the background and cannot include Screen elements; only Screen Flows support interactive screens.\n* D: This is the opposite of a Screen Flow's actual behavior — Screen Flows are defined by their interactive user screens, while autolaunched/record-triggered Flows run without any user interaction.\n* E: Screen Flows absolutely can create records (via Create Records elements), just like autolaunched Flows; record creation capability is not exclusive to record-triggered Flows."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "A nightly batch process needs to evaluate all Opportunities with a Close Date in the past that are still open, and automatically move them to a \"Stalled\" stage, running once every night regardless of any individual record being edited. Which Flow trigger type is appropriate?", options: [("A", "A Screen Flow scheduled through a calendar reminder that prompts an admin to run it manually every night."), ("B", "An autolaunched Flow that can only be triggered by a record's field change, not by a time-based schedule."), ("C", "A record-triggered Flow set to run \"before save,\" since before-save Flows can run on a nightly schedule."), ("D", "A Scheduled-Triggered Flow (Schedule-Triggered Flow) configured to run nightly, with entry criteria checking for open Opportunities whose Close Date has passed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A Schedule-Triggered Flow is specifically designed to run automatically at a defined recurring time (e.g., nightly) against a batch of records matching specified criteria, exactly matching the need to sweep all stalled Opportunities on a nightly basis regardless of individual edits. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Before-save record-triggered Flows only execute when a specific record is being saved (created or edited); they have no time-based, nightly scheduling capability independent of a save event.\n* A: A manually-run process requiring a human to remember and execute it every night is not an automated solution and defeats the purpose of building reliable automation.\n* B: This description contradicts the actual capability of Schedule-Triggered Flows, which exist precisely to provide time-based, non-record-edit-triggered automation."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A Flow needs to look up the single most recently created open Case for a given Contact, sorted by CreatedDate descending, and use only that Contact's most recent Case in its logic. What Get Records configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "A Get Records element filtered to the Contact and Case Status = Open, sorted by CreatedDate in descending order, with \"How Many Records to Store\" set to \"Only the first record.\""), ("B", "A Get Records element with no filter, since a Flow automatically returns only the single most recent record when no sort order is specified."), ("C", "A Loop element that iterates over all Cases and manually determines the most recent one using a Decision element inside the loop for every single Case in the org."), ("D", "A Roll-Up Summary field that always returns the most recently created child record's Id.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Get Records supports filtering (Contact and Status = Open), explicit sort ordering (CreatedDate descending), and a \"store only the first record\" setting — combining these three configuration options is exactly how Flow retrieves the single most recent matching record efficiently. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Get Records does not automatically limit or sort results without explicit configuration; without a defined sort order and record limit, it could return multiple records in an unpredictable order.\n* C: Looping over every Case in the org to manually determine the most recent one is extremely inefficient, risks hitting governor limits, and duplicates functionality already built into Get Records' native sorting and limiting options.\n* D: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values (SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX) from master-detail children; they cannot return or reference a specific child record's Id value."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Choose 2. An architect is reviewing a Flow that performs a Get Records call inside a Loop element, once per loop iteration, against a collection of 200 Account records to retrieve each Account's related Contacts one at a time. Which two problems does this design create?", options: [("A", "This risks exceeding the 100 SOQL queries per transaction governor limit, since each iteration of the loop performs a separate Get Records call (a SOQL query) rather than querying all needed data once outside the loop."), ("B", "This is an anti-pattern known as a \"non-bulkified\" or \"SOQL-in-a-loop\" design, and the fix is to move the Get Records call outside the loop, retrieving all needed Contacts in a single query before the loop begins."), ("C", "Get Records calls inside loops are always free and do not count against any governor limit."), ("D", "This design is required whenever more than 50 records need to be processed."), ("E", "Loops in Flow can only contain Screen elements, never Get Records elements.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Performing a Get Records (SOQL query) inside a loop for 200 iterations risks exceeding the 100 SOQL queries per transaction limit, and this is the well-known \"SOQL-in-a-loop\" anti-pattern; the fix is to query all needed related records once, before entering the loop, and then reference the already-retrieved collection inside the loop. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Get Records calls do count against the governor limit on SOQL queries per transaction; they are not free or limit-exempt, regardless of where they are placed in the Flow.\n* D: This is not a required design pattern at any record threshold; it is a documented anti-pattern for any volume of records, and there's no rule requiring per-iteration Get Records calls above 50 records — the opposite (bulkifying outside the loop) is the correct practice.\n* E: Loops in Flow can contain a wide variety of element types, including Get Records, Create Records, Update Records, Decision, and Assignment elements; they are not restricted to only Screen elements."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "A record-triggered Flow updates the same Opportunity record it was triggered from, and that update itself re-triggers the same Flow (since it's configured to run on both create and update). The business needs the second, recursive execution to be prevented cleanly. What Flow design technique correctly prevents this recursion?", options: [("A", "Recursion in record-triggered Flow is impossible by design, so no technique is ever needed."), ("B", "Add a formula or a static resource check using a custom \"Recursion Control\" checkbox field that the Flow sets to true on first execution and checks as an entry-condition exclusion on subsequent runs within the same transaction, or rely on the platform's own built-in recursion depth safeguard as a backstop."), ("C", "Convert the Flow into a Screen Flow, since Screen Flows are immune to recursion."), ("D", "Add a Pause element at the very start of the Flow to prevent it from re-triggering.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A common and effective pattern is using a recursion-control field (checked in entry criteria or a Decision element) that flags when the Flow has already run for that record in the current context, preventing a second recursive pass, with the platform's own recursion depth limit acting as an additional backstop. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Recursion in record-triggered Flow absolutely can occur — an update from the Flow re-triggering the same Flow on a subsequent update event is a well-documented risk requiring deliberate design mitigation.\n* C: Screen Flows are launched manually by user interaction and are a different Flow type entirely; converting to a Screen Flow would eliminate the automatic triggering behavior needed for this record-triggered automation use case, not \"fix\" recursion.\n* D: A Pause element introduces a wait for a specified duration or platform event; it does not detect or prevent a record update from re-triggering the same record-triggered Flow."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "A Flow's Fault Path is triggered when a Create Records element fails due to a validation rule violation on the new record. What is the correct design consideration when building the fault-handling logic connected to that Fault Path?", options: [("A", "Fault paths automatically retry the failed operation up to 3 times with no additional configuration needed."), ("B", "Fault paths can only be attached to Get Records elements, never to Create Records elements."), ("C", "The Fault Path should include logic to capture and log the error details (e.g., using the built-in $Flow.FaultMessage system variable written to a custom Error Log object) and/or notify an appropriate user, since without a Fault Path the Flow would simply fail with an unhandled, less informative error shown to the end user."), ("D", "Fault paths are purely cosmetic and have no effect on whether the Flow's error is caught or how it's communicated.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A well-designed Fault Path captures the actual error detail via $Flow.FaultMessage (often logging it to a custom object for review) and/or sends a notification, providing a controlled, informative response instead of letting the Flow fail with a generic, unhandled error shown directly to the end user. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Fault Paths do not include any automatic retry mechanism; they are simply an alternate execution branch that runs when the preceding element errors, requiring explicit logic to be built for logging, notification, or retry.\n* B: Fault Paths can be attached to nearly any DML or callout-capable element, including Create Records, Update Records, Delete Records, and Get Records — they are not restricted to Get Records only.\n* D: Fault Paths have a real functional effect — they catch the error and redirect Flow execution to alternate logic, meaningfully changing what happens and what the end user sees, rather than being purely cosmetic."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "Choose 2. A company built a Flow that performs a DML \"Update Records\" element and a separate outbound callout element within the same Flow interview, both after the triggering save. Which two statements about mixing DML and callouts in the same automation context are accurate?", options: [("A", "Apex enforces a hard rule that a callout cannot be made after uncommitted DML within the same transaction (\"you have uncommitted work pending, cannot make callout\"), and Flow's asynchronous path execution is specifically designed to avoid this by deferring the callout to a separate transaction context."), ("B", "Placing a callout in an asynchronous (scheduled) path after the triggering transaction's DML has committed avoids the DML-before-callout conflict entirely."), ("C", "DML and callouts can always be freely mixed in any order within the exact same transaction, in Flow or Apex, with zero restrictions."), ("D", "Callouts are entirely unsupported in Flow under any circumstances."), ("E", "Roll-up summary fields must be disabled temporarily whenever a callout is present in a Flow.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: The underlying platform rule prohibiting a callout after uncommitted DML in the same transaction applies to Flow's execution as well, and the standard mitigation is to defer the callout into an asynchronous path so it runs in a separate transaction context after the original DML has already committed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This is the opposite of the actual platform restriction — DML and callouts cannot be freely mixed in the same synchronous transaction context; the classic \"uncommitted work pending\" error is a well-documented governor-level restriction.\n* D: Callouts are supported in Flow, but only in appropriate contexts (such as after-save asynchronous paths); they are not universally unsupported.\n* E: Roll-up summary fields have no relationship to callout execution or DML/callout sequencing rules; disabling them is not a real or relevant mitigation technique."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "An app builder needs to decide, for a new complex multi-object business process automation requirement involving conditional branching, related record updates across three objects, and a scheduled nightly cleanup task, whether the entire solution can remain fully declarative or requires Apex. What guiding principle applies?", options: [("A", "Declarative automation is limited to a single object and a single field update per Flow, so this scenario is impossible without code."), ("B", "Any requirement touching more than one object automatically requires Apex, since Flow cannot span multiple objects."), ("C", "Apex is always required for any automation involving more than a single validation rule."), ("D", "Flow Builder today can typically handle conditional branching, multi-object record operations, and scheduled/time-based automation entirely declaratively; Apex becomes necessary primarily when requirements exceed Flow's capabilities — such as needing complex algorithmic logic beyond Flow's practical limits, tight governor-limit-sensitive bulk processing, or capabilities Flow simply doesn't expose.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Modern Flow Builder is capable of handling conditional branching, multi-object operations (via Get/Create/Update Records across related objects), and scheduled time-based triggers entirely declaratively; Apex is reserved for cases where Flow's practical limits are exceeded — highly complex algorithmic logic, extreme bulk/governor-limit-sensitive processing, or capabilities Flow doesn't natively support. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Flow absolutely can span and operate across multiple related objects using Get Records, Create Records, and Update Records elements referencing different objects within the same Flow.\n* C: This vastly overstates the necessity of Apex; validation rules aren't even part of Flow, and complex declarative automation involving branching and multi-object logic is squarely within Flow Builder's native capabilities.\n* A: Flow supports multi-object operations and multiple field updates within a single Flow; this option describes a severe, inaccurate understatement of Flow's actual declarative capability.\n\n# User Interface\n\n# User Interface"),
        Question(id: "74", question: "An Opportunity record page needs different fields visible for Sales reps versus Sales managers, without creating two separate page layouts, and the change must apply within the Lightning App Builder page itself rather than the classic layout editor. Which feature accomplishes this?", options: [("A", "Component Visibility rules on the Lightning page, configured with filter conditions referencing the running user's Profile or a custom permission, so specific components (or the fields within a Dynamic Forms-enabled field section) show or hide based on who is viewing the page."), ("B", "Field-Level Security, since it changes what's visible per record type automatically."), ("C", "A Roll-Up Summary field that hides itself for managers."), ("D", "Two separate page layouts assigned via two separate Record Types, since Component Visibility can never vary by user role or profile.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Component Visibility rules in Lightning App Builder let an app builder define filter conditions (such as User.Profile or a custom permission) that control whether a component — or with Dynamic Forms, individual fields within a section — is shown, all within a single Lightning page rather than requiring separate page layouts. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: This is the very approach the scenario says to avoid (\"without creating two separate page layouts\"), and it's also inaccurate to claim Component Visibility can never vary by profile — that's exactly one of its supported filter criteria types.\n* C: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no visibility-toggling capability tied to the viewing user's role.\n* B: Field-Level Security controls whether a field is visible/editable per profile permanently across the org, but it is not the mechanism for dynamically showing/hiding components within a single Lightning page based on context — that's Component Visibility."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "A Contact record page shows 40 fields via traditional page layout-assigned sections. The business wants specific fields to conditionally appear or hide based on other field values on the record (e.g., show \"Passport Number\" only if \"Requires Travel\" is checked), configured declaratively without a separate related record or Visualforce page. What feature enables this?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field that dynamically toggles other fields' visibility."), ("B", "Dynamic Forms, which lets individual fields be placed in a Lightning page with their own component visibility filter conditions, enabling field-level conditional display based on other field values."), ("C", "List View filters, since they control which fields display within a record page."), ("D", "Compact Layouts, since they allow conditional field visibility based on record values.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Dynamic Forms breaks individual fields out of the traditional page layout and places them directly onto the Lightning page, where each field (or field section) can have its own component visibility filter condition, enabling exactly this kind of field-level conditional display based on another field's value. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Compact Layouts control which fields display in the highlights panel and record highlights area; they have no conditional, value-based visibility logic for individual fields.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no mechanism to toggle the visibility of other fields.\n* C: List View filters control which records appear in a list view based on filter criteria; they have no bearing on field-level visibility within an individual record's detail page."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is evaluating whether to enable Dynamic Forms on an existing Contact Lightning Record Page that currently uses a traditional page layout with multiple sections. Which two facts are accurate about Dynamic Forms migration and capability?", options: [("A", "Once migrated, individual fields and field sections can each have their own component visibility filter criteria, which traditional page layout sections cannot do at the same granularity."), ("B", "Dynamic Forms is supported for custom objects and, since a broader rollout, many standard objects as well, though support and rollout have expanded over releases — the app builder should verify current support for the specific object."), ("C", "Dynamic Forms cannot coexist with Lightning App Builder pages; it requires reverting entirely to Salesforce Classic."), ("D", "Migrating to Dynamic Forms permanently and irreversibly deletes the original page layout, with no way to reference it again."), ("E", "Dynamic Forms only works for Lightning pages viewed on desktop, and fields configured this way never render on the Salesforce mobile app.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Dynamic Forms enables granular, field- and section-level component visibility filtering not available at that granularity in traditional page layouts, and while it launched for custom objects first, support has expanded to many standard objects over subsequent releases — the app builder should confirm current support for the object in question. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Dynamic Forms is itself a Lightning App Builder feature; it requires Lightning Experience and has no relationship to or dependency on Salesforce Classic.\n* D: The original page layout is not deleted; it remains intact and can still be referenced, and the migration process itself is generally reversible.\n* E: Dynamic Forms-configured fields do render on the Salesforce mobile app; this is a explicitly supported, cross-platform capability, not desktop-only."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "A Case list view needs to show, at a glance, key summary information (Case Number, Priority, Status) when displayed as a related list on an Account record page, in a more compact visual format than the full page layout. Which page layout-related feature controls this compact display?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field configured to display Case Number, Priority, and Status together as one merged value."), ("B", "A List View filter that hides all fields except the three specified."), ("C", "A Compact Layout assigned to the Case object, which defines a smaller, curated subset of fields (up to 10) shown in condensed contexts like related lists, the mobile highlights panel, and search results."), ("D", "The standard page layout's full field list, since related lists always show every field from the page layout.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A Compact Layout defines a small, curated set of fields (up to 10) specifically used in condensed display contexts — like related list rows, the highlights panel, and mobile search results — exactly matching the need for a compact, at-a-glance summary view. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Related lists do not show every field from the full page layout; that would defeat the purpose of a compact, glanceable summary and is not how related lists are configured.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children into a single number; they cannot merge multiple different fields (Case Number, Priority, Status) into a formatted compact display.\n* B: List View filters control which records appear in a list view based on criteria; they do not control which fields are shown in a condensed related list or highlights panel format."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "A sales manager wants a Kanban-style view of Opportunities grouped by Stage directly within a list view, allowing drag-and-drop stage changes. What feature provides this natively?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field that visually represents Stage as a colored bar."), ("B", "A Matrix report configured with drag-and-drop enabled."), ("C", "A Chatter feed filtered by Stage value."), ("D", "The Kanban view option available on list views for supported objects (like Opportunity), which groups records by a picklist field (like Stage) into draggable columns, letting users change a record's Stage by dragging its card.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The Kanban view is a native list view display option (for objects like Opportunity that support it) that groups records into draggable columns based on a picklist field's values, allowing users to change that field's value simply by dragging a record's card to a different column. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Reports (Matrix or otherwise) are read-only data displays; they have no drag-and-drop interactivity or ability to update records.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no visual Kanban-style board capability.\n* C: A Chatter feed shows posts and updates chronologically; it has no grouped, draggable board interface tied to a picklist field's values."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is designing a Lightning App (the app itself, via App Manager) for a Field Service team, distinct from a single Lightning record page. Which two configuration capabilities are specifically part of building a Lightning App (not a single record page)?", options: [("A", "Defining the app's set of navigation items (tabs), such as which objects, Lightning pages, and Visualforce pages appear in the app's navigation bar."), ("B", "Restricting the app's visibility and assignment to specific user profiles or permission sets, so only relevant users see this app in the App Launcher."), ("C", "Setting field-level security for every field on every object used anywhere in the org."), ("D", "Defining the page layout assigned to a specific record type."), ("E", "Configuring Duplicate Rules for Leads.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Configuring a Lightning App's navigation items (tabs) and restricting its visibility/assignment to specific profiles or permission sets are both core, defining configuration steps specifically performed when building the App itself in App Manager, distinct from configuring an individual record page. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Field-level security is configured per profile/permission set at the object-field level, entirely independent of any specific Lightning App's configuration.\n* D: Page layout assignment to record types is an object-level configuration done in Object Manager, not part of building the Lightning App itself.\n* E: Duplicate Rules are a data-quality feature configured independently of any Lightning App and have no relationship to app navigation or app-level configuration."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "A custom button needs to open a pre-populated New Task form with the Subject field already filled in as \"Follow-up Call\" and the WhatId set to the current record, without writing any Apex or Visualforce. What declarative approach achieves this?", options: [("A", "A custom button using a URL formula with a New Task URL pattern and pre-populated field parameters passed via query string parameters (e.g., using the appropriate field IDs), OR a Quick Action of type \"Create a Record\" for Task with predefined field values set declaratively."), ("B", "A Roll-Up Summary field that automatically creates Task records with a fixed Subject value."), ("C", "A validation rule that pre-fills the Subject field on any new Task."), ("D", "This absolutely requires an Apex-based Visualforce page; there is no declarative way to pre-populate a new record form.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Both a custom button using a URL formula with pre-population query parameters and a declarative \"Create a Record\" Quick Action with predefined field values are native, code-free ways to pre-populate a new Task's fields (like Subject and WhatId) when the user opens the form. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no capability to create new Task records or pre-populate any form field.\n* C: A validation rule can only block or allow a save based on a formula condition; it has no capability to pre-fill a field's value when a new record form is opened.\n* D: This is factually incorrect — both URL button pre-population and declarative Quick Actions with predefined values are well-established, fully declarative solutions for this exact requirement."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "A company needs a custom link on the Account page layout that, when clicked, opens Google Maps in a new browser tab centered on the Account's Billing Address, using the address fields dynamically. What button/link configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "A validation rule that redirects to Google Maps upon save."), ("B", "A custom link with Content Source set to \"URL,\" configured to open in a new window, using a formula that concatenates a Google Maps base URL with the Account's BillingStreet, BillingCity, BillingState, and BillingPostalCode merge fields."), ("C", "A Roll-Up Summary field that stores a static Google Maps link."), ("D", "A List View that filters Accounts by Billing Address.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A custom link with its Content Source set to URL supports a formula that dynamically concatenates the Account's actual Billing Address merge fields into a properly formatted Google Maps URL, and setting it to open in a new window satisfies the \"new browser tab\" requirement, entirely declaratively. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they cannot store or generate a dynamic, per-record URL string.\n* A: Validation rules can only block or allow a save based on a formula; they have no capability to open external URLs or redirect a browser.\n* D: A List View filters and displays a list of records matching criteria; it has no mechanism to generate or open a dynamic external URL for an individual record's address."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "A requirement calls for a fully custom, highly interactive drag-and-drop interface embedded directly on a Lightning record page, with client-side logic and real-time UI updates that go well beyond what standard components and Flow screens can offer. Which technology is the correct, currently-recommended choice, and why not Aura?", options: [("A", "An Aura component, since Aura is a newer and more actively developed framework than LWC."), ("B", "A standard Screen Flow, since Flow can always achieve any level of custom drag-and-drop interactivity Apex/JS code can."), ("C", "A Lightning Web Component (LWC), since it is Salesforce's modern, standards-based, currently-recommended framework for building custom Lightning components, generally offering better performance than the older Aura framework, which is a legacy approach Salesforce recommends against for new development."), ("D", "A Visualforce page, since Visualforce is the currently-recommended technology for embedding highly interactive components inside Lightning record pages.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Lightning Web Components are Salesforce's modern, web-standards-based programming model and are the currently recommended framework for building custom UI components, generally delivering better performance than the older Aura framework, which Salesforce no longer recommends for new development. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This reverses the actual relationship — Aura is the older, legacy framework, and LWC is the newer one that Salesforce actively recommends for new component development.\n* D: Visualforce is an older page technology primarily suited to full-page custom UI in Salesforce Classic-style contexts; it is not the currently-recommended approach for embedding interactive components inside modern Lightning record pages, where LWC is preferred.\n* B: Standard Screen Flow components have real limits on custom interactivity (like complex drag-and-drop with client-side real-time logic); highly custom interactive behavior beyond Flow's built-in components is exactly the boundary where LWC development becomes necessary."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is deciding when a requirement crosses the line from \"declarative Lightning page configuration\" into needing programmatic development (LWC/Aura). Which two scenarios genuinely require programmatic development rather than declarative configuration alone?", options: [("A", "Displaying a real-time, continuously polling external stock price ticker widget embedded directly within a record page with custom visual styling."), ("B", "Building a highly custom, non-standard interactive data visualization (e.g., a custom animated org-chart-style relationship diagram) that no standard or Flow-based component can render."), ("C", "Rearranging existing standard components (Related List, Chatter feed, Activities) on a Lightning record page using drag-and-drop in App Builder."), ("D", "Adding a Rich Text component with static formatted instructions to a Lightning page."), ("E", "Creating a new validation rule to enforce a required field.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A continuously polling real-time external widget and a highly custom, non-standard interactive visualization both exceed the capabilities of standard Lightning components and Flow screens, genuinely requiring custom LWC/Aura development to implement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Rearranging existing standard components via drag-and-drop in App Builder is the definition of declarative page configuration, requiring no code at all.\n* D: Adding a Rich Text component with static content is a basic, fully declarative Lightning App Builder configuration task.\n* E: Creating a validation rule is a standard declarative configuration task entirely unrelated to page/component development, requiring no code."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "A sales team wants a custom \"Log Site Visit\" action button that appears in the mobile action bar for Contacts, launching a Screen Flow rather than a simple record edit form, because it must perform conditional logic and create two related records. Which action type should be created?", options: [("A", "A standard \"New\" Quick Action, since it always supports launching a Flow."), ("B", "A List Button, since list buttons are the only action type available on mobile."), ("C", "A Custom Link with Content Source set to \"Formula Text,\" since formula text links can launch Flows directly."), ("D", "A Flow Action (Quick Action of type \"Flow\"), specifically referencing the Screen Flow, added to the object's page layout's mobile/Lightning Experience Actions section so it appears in the mobile action bar.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A Flow-type Quick Action is specifically designed to launch a designated Screen Flow, and adding it to the page layout's Actions section makes it appear in the mobile action bar, satisfying both the \"conditional logic + multiple record creation\" need (handled inside the Flow) and the mobile visibility requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A standard \"New\" Quick Action creates a single new record using a simple form; it does not launch a Flow or support the conditional multi-record logic described.\n* B: List Buttons operate on multiple selected records in a list view context; they are not the mechanism for a single-record mobile action bar item, and mobile action bars support several action types, not solely list buttons.\n* C: A Custom Link with Content Source \"Formula Text\" generates a static or dynamic text/URL output; it has no capability to launch a Screen Flow interactively."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is comparing List Views and Reports for a specific need: reps want to quickly filter and inline-edit up to 200 of their own open Opportunities directly within the object's tab, without navigating to Report Builder. Which two capabilities specifically favor List Views over Reports for this use case?", options: [("A", "List Views support inline editing of field values directly within the grid without opening each record individually."), ("B", "List Views can be scoped with a filter like \"My Opportunities\" and refined further with additional filter criteria, directly accessible from the object tab with minimal clicks."), ("C", "Reports support grouping data into multiple levels of subtotals, which List Views cannot do at all."), ("D", "Reports natively support inline record editing directly within the report grid by default."), ("E", "List Views can include cross-object joined data blocks the way joined reports can.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: List Views uniquely support direct inline editing within the grid and are quickly accessible and filterable (e.g., \"My Opportunities\" plus custom filters) right from the object tab — both squarely matching the described quick-filter, quick-edit use case better suited to List Views than Reports. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: While Reports do support subtotal grouping, this is being offered as a reason to prefer Reports, which doesn't address why List Views are favored for this specific inline-editing use case, and it isn't relevant to the stated requirement.\n* D: Reports do not natively support inline editing within the report grid by default; this is actually a defining advantage of List Views, not Reports, making this statement inaccurate as a Report capability.\n* E: Joined report blocks are a Report-specific capability; List Views have no equivalent cross-object joined block feature."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "A support team wants their Case list view to visually flag high-priority cases with a distinct background row color when Priority equals \"Critical,\" directly within the list view grid, without writing any code. What supports this?", options: [("A", "This specific capability (native conditional row-color formatting within standard List Views) is not available out-of-the-box in standard Lightning list views; achieving this typically requires a custom Lightning Web Component list view replacement or an AppExchange solution, since standard list views do not offer built-in conditional row coloring."), ("B", "List views support native conditional row-highlighting/formatting rules configured declaratively in the list view setup, directly coloring rows based on field criteria like Priority = Critical."), ("C", "A Roll-Up Summary field can be configured to color rows based on Priority."), ("D", "A validation rule can apply background row coloring conditionally within a list view.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Standard Lightning list views do not natively support conditional row-level background coloring based on field values; this visual capability typically requires a custom LWC-based list view replacement or an AppExchange package, since it exceeds what out-of-the-box list view configuration offers. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This describes a capability standard list views do not actually have; there is no native, declarative conditional row-coloring configuration option built into standard Lightning list view setup.\n* C: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no visual formatting or row-coloring capability whatsoever.\n* D: Validation rules can only block or allow record saves based on a formula; they have no capability to apply visual formatting to a list view display."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A Lightning page for the Service Console needs a component that shows only when the user viewing the record has the \"Escalation_Manager\" permission set assigned, hiding it from everyone else, entirely through declarative configuration. What is the correct configuration?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field that checks the user's permission set assignment."), ("B", "Component Visibility filter on the component, using a filter condition referencing a custom permission tied to (or the permission set assignment reflected through) User criteria, so the component only renders for users meeting that permission condition."), ("C", "Field-Level Security set on the component's underlying field to restrict visibility by permission set."), ("D", "A page layout assigned specifically to users with the Escalation_Manager permission set.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Component Visibility filters can reference user-based criteria, including custom permissions (which are commonly granted via permission sets), allowing a component to be declaratively shown only to users who meet that specific permission condition. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Field-Level Security controls field visibility/editability at the object-field level; it is not the mechanism for controlling whether an entire Lightning page component renders based on a user's permission set.\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no capability to evaluate a user's permission set assignment or control component rendering.\n* D: Page layouts are assigned via profile and record type combinations, not permission set assignments, and layout assignment doesn't provide the granular, single-component show/hide behavior described."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "Choose 2. An app builder is deciding between using a Lightning Page's List View component versus a Related List component when embedding Opportunity data on an Account record page. Which two considerations are relevant to this decision?", options: [("A", "A Related List component specifically shows records related to the current record via a defined relationship (e.g., Opportunities related to this Account), automatically scoped to that relationship."), ("B", "A List View component can display any existing list view definition for an object, but it is not automatically scoped to the current record's relationships unless the list view itself is filtered accordingly (and even then, filtering by \"the current record\" isn't native to standard list view filter criteria)."), ("C", "Related List components can never be added to a Lightning Record Page; they are exclusive to Classic page layouts."), ("D", "List View components automatically inherit the current record's Id as an implicit filter with zero configuration."), ("E", "Related List components support inline editing of related record fields directly, similar to standard list views.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A Related List component is inherently scoped to records related to the current record through a defined relationship, while a List View component displays a standalone list view definition that isn't automatically scoped to the current record unless deliberately designed to approximate that (which standard list view filters don't natively support well). Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Related List components are a standard, fully supported Lightning Record Page component type; they are not exclusive to Classic page layouts.\n* D: List View components do not automatically inherit or filter by the current record's Id; this is precisely why they are not automatically scoped the way Related List components are.\n* E: While related lists in Lightning do support some inline editing, framing it as a differentiating consideration for this specific List-View-vs-Related-List decision misrepresents the more fundamental relationship-scoping distinction that actually drives this choice, which is captured correctly in A and B."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A global action needs to let users create a new Contact from anywhere in the app, but must automatically set the Contact's LeadSource field to \"Referral\" without showing that field on the entry form at all. What Quick Action configuration achieves this?", options: [("A", "A validation rule that forces LeadSource to always equal \"Referral\" for every new Contact org-wide."), ("B", "A global \"Create a Record\" Quick Action for Contact with LeadSource placed on the layout and marked read-only, showing \"Referral\" to the user without letting them edit it."), ("C", "A global \"Create a Record\" Quick Action for Contact, with LeadSource set as a Predefined Field Value (not placed on the action's layout), so it's set automatically behind the scenes and never shown to the user."), ("D", "A Roll-Up Summary field that automatically sets LeadSource to \"Referral.\"")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Predefined Field Values on a Quick Action set a field's value automatically upon record creation without that field needing to appear on the action's layout at all, exactly matching the requirement to set LeadSource silently with no visible field on the form. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This still places the field on the layout for the user to see (even if read-only), directly contradicting the requirement that the field \"not show on the entry form at all.\"\n* A: A validation rule forcing LeadSource to always equal \"Referral\" for every new Contact org-wide would incorrectly apply to all Contact creation paths, not just this specific global action, and wrongly prevents any legitimately different LeadSource value elsewhere.\n* D: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no capability to set a picklist field's value on record creation."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "A Lightning page needs a component visible only to users viewing the page on a mobile device (phone form factor), hidden entirely when viewed on desktop. What Component Visibility filter option supports this?", options: [("A", "A filter condition referencing the user's Profile, since mobile users always have a distinct profile from desktop users."), ("B", "A Roll-Up Summary field that detects screen resolution."), ("C", "There is no way to conditionally show a component based on device type; a separate mobile-only app must be built entirely from scratch instead."), ("D", "A Component Visibility filter condition based on \"Form Factor\" (equals Phone or Small), a built-in filter criterion specifically designed to detect the device type/screen size the user is currently viewing the page on.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Component Visibility filters include a built-in \"Form Factor\" criterion that directly detects whether the user is viewing the page on a Large (desktop), Medium, or Small/Phone form factor, making it the correct native way to show a component only on mobile phone form factors. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: User profiles are unrelated to device type — the same user with the same profile can view a page on both desktop and mobile, so profile-based filtering cannot reliably distinguish device form factor.\n* B: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail children; they have no capability to detect a viewing device's screen resolution or form factor.\n* C: This is inaccurate — Form Factor-based Component Visibility is a native, built-in capability specifically for this exact use case, requiring no separate app or code.\n\n# App Deployment\n\n# App Deployment"),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A development team needs a sandbox that includes a full copy of production data (all records) for realistic UAT testing before a major release, refreshed roughly once per quarter. Which sandbox type is appropriate, and what is its storage limit consideration?", options: [("A", "A Full sandbox, which is the only sandbox type that replicates the entire production org including all data (not just metadata/configuration), with storage matching production size, but limited to a minimum refresh interval of 29 days."), ("B", "A Developer sandbox, since Developer sandboxes always include a full copy of production data."), ("C", "A Developer Pro sandbox, since it includes full production data with no storage limit at all."), ("D", "A Partial Copy sandbox, since it always includes 100% of production's data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A Full sandbox is the only sandbox type that copies the entire production org including all data and metadata, matching production's storage size, and it has a minimum refresh interval of 29 days — correctly matching the described quarterly full-data UAT need. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Developer sandboxes copy only metadata/configuration, not any production data, and have a small storage limit (200 MB); they explicitly do not include a full data copy.\n* C: Developer Pro sandboxes also copy only metadata (no production data) and have a modest storage limit (1 GB); they do not include full data and do have a storage ceiling.\n* D: Partial Copy sandboxes include only a sample of production data (up to 10,000 records per selected object via a sandbox template), not the full data set, which contradicts the \"full copy of production data\" requirement."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "Choose 2. A team is choosing between a Developer sandbox and a Developer Pro sandbox for ongoing feature development work. Which two facts accurately distinguish these two sandbox types?", options: [("A", "Developer sandboxes have a 200 MB storage limit, while Developer Pro sandboxes have a larger 1 GB storage limit, making Developer Pro more suitable for larger data/metadata volumes during development."), ("B", "Both Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes copy only metadata from production, not production data, and both can be refreshed once per day (a 1-day refresh interval)."), ("C", "Developer Pro sandboxes include a full copy of production's actual data records, while Developer sandboxes never include any data."), ("D", "Developer sandboxes support a 29-day minimum refresh interval, same as Full sandboxes."), ("E", "Only Developer Pro sandboxes can be used for Apex unit testing; Developer sandboxes cannot run Apex tests at all.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Developer sandboxes are capped at 200 MB while Developer Pro sandboxes offer a larger 1 GB storage allowance, and both sandbox types copy only metadata (no production data) and share the same fast, 1-day minimum refresh interval, making both facts accurate distinguishing/shared characteristics. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Neither Developer nor Developer Pro sandboxes include actual production data records; both copy only metadata/configuration, contradicting the claim that Developer Pro includes a full data copy.\n* D: Developer sandboxes have a 1-day minimum refresh interval, not 29 days; the 29-day minimum refresh interval applies to Full sandboxes, not Developer sandboxes.\n* E: Both Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes fully support running Apex unit tests; this capability is not restricted to only one of the two sandbox types."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "A QA team needs a sandbox with a representative sample of production data (not the full data set) for functional testing of a new feature, refreshed roughly every 5 days as development iterates. Which sandbox type fits, and what refresh constraint applies?", options: [("A", "A Partial Copy sandbox, but it can only ever be refreshed once, never again after initial creation."), ("B", "A Partial Copy sandbox, which includes a sample of production data (via a sandbox template, up to 10,000 records per selected object) but has a minimum refresh interval of 5 days, making a roughly-every-5-days cadence exactly compatible."), ("C", "A Developer sandbox, since Developer sandboxes support a 5-day sample data refresh natively."), ("D", "A Full sandbox, since only Full sandboxes can include any sample data at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A Partial Copy sandbox is specifically designed to include a representative sample of production data via a sandbox template (up to 10,000 records per selected object) and has a minimum refresh interval of 5 days, which aligns exactly with the team's roughly-every-5-days refresh cadence. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* D: Full sandboxes are not the only type that can include sample data — Partial Copy sandboxes are specifically the sample-data-focused sandbox type, while Full sandboxes copy the entire data set, not a sample.\n* C: Developer sandboxes copy only metadata with no production data at all, sample or otherwise, and have a different (1-day) refresh interval than described.\n* A: Partial Copy sandboxes can be refreshed repeatedly over time, subject to their minimum refresh interval; they are not limited to a single one-time refresh."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "A release manager needs to move a set of custom fields, a Flow, and a Lightning page from a sandbox to production, but the change set keeps failing validation because a dependent custom field referenced by the Flow was left out of the outbound change set. What does this scenario illustrate about change sets?", options: [("A", "Change sets can bypass component dependency requirements entirely if the org has API access enabled."), ("B", "Change sets automatically include all dependencies with zero manual effort, so this failure indicates a random system error unrelated to configuration."), ("C", "Change sets require the release manager to manually identify and explicitly add all dependent components (such as fields referenced by a Flow) into the outbound change set; missing dependencies is a common, well-documented change set limitation and failure cause."), ("D", "Change sets can only deploy Apex classes, never Flows, Lightning pages, or custom fields.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Change sets require manually adding every needed component, and unlike some other deployment tools they do not automatically resolve and include all dependencies for you; a missing dependent field referenced by an included Flow is one of the most common, well-documented causes of change set validation failures. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is the opposite of the actual limitation — change sets specifically do NOT auto-include all dependencies, which is exactly why the release manager must manually add every dependent component.\n* D: Change sets support a broad range of metadata component types including custom fields, Flows, and Lightning pages; they are not restricted to Apex classes only.\n* A: There is no such bypass mechanism tied to API access; dependency validation is a structural requirement of the change set deployment process regardless of API settings."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "Choose 2. An architect is evaluating change sets as a deployment tool for an upcoming multi-sandbox release pipeline (Dev → QA → UAT → Production). Which two limitations of change sets are relevant considerations for this pipeline design?", options: [("A", "Change sets can only be deployed between organizations that have an established, explicit deployment connection (e.g., a sandbox and its specific production org, or related sandboxes within the same hierarchy) — not between arbitrary, unconnected orgs."), ("B", "Change sets provide no native version control history or rollback mechanism; if a deployment introduces a defect, there is no built-in \"undo\" — a corrective change set or manual fix must be deployed instead."), ("C", "Change sets automatically run all Apex tests required for production deployment with zero configuration needed."), ("D", "Change sets can deploy directly between any two arbitrary sandboxes in any other company's org, regardless of connection."), ("E", "Change sets require no dependency management of any kind, since the platform resolves all dependencies invisibly.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Change sets require an explicit, established deployment connection between the source and target orgs, and they lack any native rollback/version-control mechanism, meaning defects require a corrective follow-up deployment — both are genuine, relevant limitations for planning a multi-sandbox release pipeline. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Apex test execution requirements for production deployment are governed by Salesforce's platform-level code coverage rules, not something change sets \"automatically run\" as a built-in convenience feature; sufficient existing test coverage must already exist.\n* D: Change sets cannot deploy between unconnected orgs, let alone across different companies' orgs; this directly contradicts the actual, restrictive connection requirement.\n* E: As established, change sets specifically require manual dependency management — the platform does not invisibly resolve all dependencies for you."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "A software vendor wants to distribute an app to multiple customer orgs via AppExchange, needs to push upgrades to all installed customer orgs later, and wants to protect their Apex code from being viewed or modified by installing customers. Which packaging approach is correct?", options: [("A", "Neither packaging type supports hiding Apex code; this always requires a support ticket with Salesforce to obfuscate code manually."), ("B", "An unmanaged package, since unmanaged packages support push upgrades and hide Apex code from installers by default."), ("C", "A change set, since change sets can be distributed via AppExchange to any org."), ("D", "A managed package, since managed packages support versioned upgrades that can be pushed to subscriber orgs, and their Apex code is compiled/hidden from installers, unlike unmanaged packages where all components remain fully open and editable after install.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Managed packages are specifically designed for ISV-style distribution — supporting versioned, pushable upgrades to subscriber orgs and protecting the vendor's Apex code (hidden/compiled) from being viewed or modified by installing customers, exactly matching all three stated needs. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Unmanaged packages install all components as fully open, editable, unlocked metadata in the target org with no code protection and no push-upgrade capability — the opposite of what's described.\n* C: Change sets are an intra-org-connection deployment tool tied to specific connected orgs; they are not distributed via AppExchange to arbitrary customer orgs at all.\n* A: This is inaccurate — managed packages natively and directly support Apex code protection as a built-in packaging feature, requiring no special support ticket or manual obfuscation process."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "Choose 2. A team unit-testing a new managed package feature realizes an unmanaged package was actually a poor choice for their original use case, since they now need to push a critical bug fix update to all five customers who already installed the original version. Which two facts explain why they should have used a managed package instead?", options: [("A", "Unmanaged packages have no concept of package versioning or a push-upgrade mechanism; once installed, all components become standalone, disconnected metadata in the subscriber org, with no way to centrally update them all at once."), ("B", "Managed packages support incremental version upgrades that can be pushed out to all subscriber orgs from the developer org, exactly enabling the desired centralized bug-fix distribution."), ("C", "Unmanaged packages actually do support push upgrades, but only for Apex classes, not for any other metadata type."), ("D", "Managed packages cannot include Apex code, so the bug fix would need to be delivered as a separate change set regardless."), ("E", "Both package types support push upgrades identically; the real issue was unrelated to packaging type.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Unmanaged packages leave installed components as disconnected, standalone metadata with no versioning or push-upgrade mechanism, while managed packages are specifically built to support incremental versioned upgrades pushed centrally to all subscriber orgs — exactly the capability gap that explains the team's problem. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Unmanaged packages do not support push upgrades at all, for any metadata type, including Apex classes; this is a defining limitation, not a partial capability.\n* D: Managed packages absolutely can and commonly do include Apex code as a core part of their package contents; this claim is factually incorrect.\n* E: This directly contradicts the fundamental difference between the two packaging types — push upgrade capability is one of the most significant distinguishing features, present in managed packages and absent in unmanaged packages."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "A release manager is planning the deployment sequence for a release that includes: a new custom object with a master-detail relationship to an existing object, a validation rule on the new object, a Flow that creates records on the new object, and a permission set granting access to the new object. What sequencing principle must guide the deployment plan?", options: [("A", "Dependent components must be deployed in an order (or as part of a single change set/package where Salesforce's own dependency resolution applies within that deployment) that respects their dependency chain — the custom object and its relationship must exist before the validation rule, Flow, and permission set that reference it can be meaningfully validated and deployed successfully."), ("B", "All four components can be deployed in a single change set in any arbitrary order, since Salesforce always resolves ordering internally regardless of dependency chains."), ("C", "The permission set must always be deployed before the custom object it references, since permission sets are metadata-independent of the objects they grant access to."), ("D", "Validation rules must always be deployed in a completely separate deployment from the object they apply to, per platform requirement.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Deployment sequencing (whether via ordered change sets or a bundled deployment where Salesforce resolves in-deployment dependencies) must respect the underlying dependency chain — the custom object and its relationship must exist before dependent components referencing it (validation rule, Flow, permission set) can be successfully validated and deployed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: While Salesforce does resolve dependencies within a single deployment/change set transaction when all components are included together, this doesn't mean order is arbitrary or irrelevant across separate deployments — dependency chains still fundamentally govern what can succeed and when.\n* C: A permission set granting access to a custom object is dependent on that object's existence; claiming it's \"metadata-independent\" of the object it references misstates the actual dependency relationship.\n* D: There is no platform requirement mandating that validation rules always be deployed in a fully separate deployment from their object; they are commonly deployed together within the same change set or package as long as dependencies are satisfied."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "A company is planning an Application Lifecycle Management (ALM) approach for ongoing development, choosing between change sets and a more robust source-driven development tool (e.g., Salesforce CLI/DX with version control) for a growing team of 8 developers working in parallel. What ALM consideration favors the more robust tooling?", options: [("A", "Change sets are always faster to configure than any CLI-based deployment, regardless of team size, so they remain the superior choice for any team size."), ("B", "A source-driven development approach (using version control, e.g., Git, alongside Salesforce CLI) provides proper version history, branching/merging for parallel work streams, and repeatable, scriptable deployments — capabilities change sets lack, becoming increasingly valuable as team size and deployment frequency grow."), ("C", "Change sets natively provide full version control history, branching, and merge conflict resolution across a large parallel development team, matching what dedicated source-driven tools offer."), ("D", "ALM tooling choice has no bearing on team size; the two approaches are functionally identical for any number of developers.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A source-driven approach using version control alongside Salesforce CLI provides real version history, branching and merging for parallel work, and repeatable scripted deployments — all capabilities that change sets fundamentally lack and that become progressively more valuable as a development team and its parallel workstreams grow. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Change sets have no native version control history, branching, or merge conflict resolution capability at all; this is precisely the gap that source-driven tools with Git fill.\n* A: While change sets may feel simpler for very small, infrequent deployments, this doesn't hold as team size and deployment complexity grow, and the claim of universal superiority regardless of team size is inaccurate.\n* D: Team size and deployment frequency are significant, well-documented factors in ALM tooling decisions; claiming no bearing at all misrepresents standard ALM guidance."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "A Partial Copy sandbox refresh is scheduled, but the release manager discovers the sandbox has been customized directly by QA testers with configuration changes that were never captured in version control or a change set. What ALM risk does this illustrate, and what governance practice would have prevented it?", options: [("A", "Partial Copy sandboxes are immune to data loss on refresh because they always merge old and new configurations automatically."), ("B", "This is not a real risk, since sandbox refreshes never affect any existing metadata or configuration in a sandbox."), ("C", "Refreshing the sandbox will overwrite it entirely with a fresh copy from production, permanently losing any uncaptured configuration changes; the preventive governance practice is enforcing that all sandbox configuration changes be captured in change sets or version control (source-driven development) before any refresh, as part of a disciplined release management process."), ("D", "The correct fix is to disable all future sandbox refreshes permanently to avoid this risk entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A sandbox refresh replaces the sandbox's entire contents with a fresh copy from production (or its source), meaning any configuration changes made directly in that sandbox and never captured elsewhere are permanently lost — the correct governance practice is disciplined capture of all changes into change sets or version control before any refresh occurs. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is factually incorrect — a sandbox refresh explicitly replaces the sandbox's existing metadata and data with a new copy, which is exactly why uncaptured local changes get lost.\n* A: Sandbox refreshes do not automatically merge old and new configurations; the refresh process is a replacement, not a merge, of the target sandbox's contents.\n* D: Permanently disabling all sandbox refreshes isn't a practical or intended governance solution; sandboxes need periodic refreshing to stay aligned with production, and the real fix is disciplined change capture, not eliminating refreshes altogether.\n</data_ad17c01eccedb2571f7edbd7>")
    ]
